And you are an hour behind schedule.

249
00:12:11,086 --> 00:12:12,759
- [Crow] Hey, he flipped him off.

250
00:12:12,759 --> 00:12:14,154
- [Tom] We're gonna have
to jettison your mustache,

251
00:12:14,154 --> 00:12:15,154
you realize.

252
00:12:16,037 --> 00:12:17,187
- He we go then.

253
00:12:17,187 --> 00:12:21,348
("Moon Zero Two" theme music)

254
00:12:21,348 --> 00:12:23,912
- [Tom] Hey look, there
is a man in the Moon.

255
00:12:23,912 --> 00:12:27,745
("Moon Zero Two" theme music)

256
00:12:37,470 --> 00:12:40,686
- SpaceWay announces
the arrival of flight.--

257
00:12:40,686 --> 00:12:42,168
- [Crow] There's a
space blue-light special

258
00:12:42,168 --> 00:12:43,773
on quadrant B9.

259
00:12:43,773 --> 00:12:46,250
- If you're Moon Zero Two,
I wish you'd sell that thing

260
00:12:46,250 --> 00:12:48,676
along with all the other
space rubbish you collect.

261
00:12:48,676 --> 00:12:50,812
- [Tom] Don't say hi.

262
00:12:50,812 --> 00:12:52,142
- I don't think I caught the name.

263
00:12:52,142 --> 00:12:53,938
- I'm the second officer
of the Moon Express.

264
00:12:53,938 --> 00:12:56,180
You delayed us for nearly two minutes.

265
00:12:56,180 --> 00:12:58,974
- If you took off on time,
you might land on time.

266
00:12:58,974 --> 00:12:59,807
- I don't see that my.

267
00:12:59,807 --> 00:13:01,990
- I though you were seeing our
passengers through customs.

268
00:13:01,990 --> 00:13:03,073
- I was just telling this man.

269
00:13:03,073 --> 00:13:05,440
- You can't tell this man
anything about anything.

270
00:13:05,440 --> 00:13:06,957
- [Crow] But feel free
to tell him something

271
00:13:06,957 --> 00:13:10,640
about nothing or a little about a lot.

272
00:13:10,640 --> 00:13:12,174
- [Joel] You'll have to excuse my friend.

273
00:13:12,174 --> 00:13:14,330
He's kind of a weenie.

274
00:13:14,330 --> 00:13:17,159
- Thank God not all of them make it.

275
00:13:17,159 --> 00:13:20,272
Dan, get this over to the scrapyard.

276
00:13:20,272 --> 00:13:22,486
Oh, with your permission.

277
00:13:22,486 --> 00:13:23,386
And don't take anything less

278
00:13:23,386 --> 00:13:25,487
than 12,000 Moon dollars for it.

279
00:13:25,487 --> 00:13:26,678
- I'll try.

280
00:13:26,678 --> 00:13:28,718
- I've just got time for a shower.

281
00:13:28,718 --> 00:13:30,282
- I wanna talk to you, though.

282
00:13:30,282 --> 00:13:31,759
- Go ahead.

283
00:13:31,759 --> 00:13:32,660
- My name is Taplin.

284
00:13:32,660 --> 00:13:34,779
- Please, turn and face
immigration identification

285
00:13:34,779 --> 00:13:36,157
computer, please.

286
00:13:36,157 --> 00:13:39,053
- [Crow] I'm here for the Gumby wedding.

287
00:13:39,053 --> 00:13:39,886
- [Tom] Test your love.

288
00:13:39,886 --> 00:13:41,136
Flaming, great.

289
00:13:42,185 --> 00:13:43,018
- Is there a message for me.

290
00:13:43,018 --> 00:13:44,843
- Message desk's over there.

291
00:13:44,843 --> 00:13:45,843
- Thank you.

292
00:13:47,146 --> 00:13:48,444
- Good evening, Mr. Hubbard.

293
00:13:48,444 --> 00:13:50,611
- [Crow] Wow, it's Castro.

294
00:13:52,026 --> 00:13:53,966
- I'm sorry Mr. Hubbard.

295
00:13:53,966 --> 00:13:55,392
I hope you enjoyed your trip, sir.

296
00:13:55,392 --> 00:13:57,984
- No more or less than 100
other trips I've taken.

297
00:13:57,984 --> 00:14:00,567
- [Crow] Where's the Armor All?

298
00:14:02,744 --> 00:14:04,556
- [Bill] That's a nifty little charmer.

299
00:14:04,556 --> 00:14:06,174
- J.J. Hubbard?

300
00:14:06,174 --> 00:14:07,932
- So, that's 100% Hubbard.

301
00:14:07,932 --> 00:14:09,148
Is he always like that?

302
00:14:09,148 --> 00:14:10,744
- Yes, sir, to the likes of us.

303
00:14:10,744 --> 00:14:12,072
- [Tom] Sometimes he's 50%.

304
00:14:12,072 --> 00:14:13,202
- What does he want up here?

305
00:14:13,202 --> 00:14:14,103
- Oh, I don't know.

306
00:14:14,103 --> 00:14:16,168
- [Tim] Are you getting shorter, Bill?

307
00:14:16,168 --> 00:14:19,014
- If it was mine he could have it.

308
00:14:19,014 --> 00:14:19,847
Cheap.

309
00:14:22,092 --> 00:14:25,793
- Listen, I was talking to
personnel back at Earth Base.

310
00:14:25,793 --> 00:14:28,111
(whooshing)

311
00:14:28,111 --> 00:14:30,069
And he'd like you back
in the corporation, Bill.

312
00:14:30,069 --> 00:14:31,653
- Are they starting
exploration flights again?

313
00:14:31,653 --> 00:14:32,909
- No.

314
00:14:32,909 --> 00:14:34,419
- [Crow] No, sorting boxes.

315
00:14:34,419 --> 00:14:35,959
- When they outfit the
first flight to Mercury,

316
00:14:35,959 --> 00:14:38,038
tell them to call me.

317
00:14:38,038 --> 00:14:40,820
- Come on Bill, you know
the answer to that one.

318
00:14:40,820 --> 00:14:42,008
- [Tom] They don't have your number.

319
00:14:42,008 --> 00:14:43,062
It's not listed.

320
00:14:43,062 --> 00:14:45,666
- To line the rocket ships
for journeys like that.

321
00:14:45,666 --> 00:14:48,876
We've got regular flights
to Mars and Venus.

322
00:14:48,876 --> 00:14:50,216
What more do you want?

323
00:14:50,216 --> 00:14:52,126
- I'm not going back into the corporation.

324
00:14:52,126 --> 00:14:54,911
- [Joel] Did he get that shirt
at a Molly Hatchet concert?

325
00:14:54,911 --> 00:14:58,387
- Not a mechanically minded wet nurse.

326
00:14:58,387 --> 00:15:00,220
- Thank you very much.

327
00:15:01,321 --> 00:15:03,059
(coughing)

328
00:15:03,059 --> 00:15:06,109
someone has to be a passenger pilot.

329
00:15:06,109 --> 00:15:07,665
- What's that you say?

330
00:15:07,665 --> 00:15:12,113
- I said, "Someone has
to be a passenger pilot."

331
00:15:12,113 --> 00:15:13,446
- Can't hear ya.

332
00:15:15,201 --> 00:15:16,663
- Forget it.

333
00:15:16,663 --> 00:15:20,830
- [Crow] I'll go get the
rest of your shower door.

334
00:15:23,941 --> 00:15:25,481
- [Tom] Hey, Thumblina.

335
00:15:25,481 --> 00:15:28,883
- Sorry miss, this area's
out-of-bounds to passengers.

336
00:15:28,883 --> 00:15:30,101
- I'm looking for Mr.

337
00:15:30,101 --> 00:15:31,043
- [Tom] Goodbar?

338
00:15:31,043 --> 00:15:32,293
- Captain Kemp.

339
00:15:34,051 --> 00:15:36,218
- Oh, well, he's in there.

340
00:15:37,313 --> 00:15:40,239
He's doing something just at the moment.

341
00:15:40,239 --> 00:15:41,989
But he won't be long.

342
00:15:44,047 --> 00:15:47,011
- [Crow] Hey, your calculator's on.

343
00:15:47,011 --> 00:15:49,928
- [Joel] Oh boy, is she ever gonna.

344
00:15:51,152 --> 00:15:52,609
- [Tom] Hey, what are you looking at?

345
00:15:52,609 --> 00:15:53,442
Whoops.

346
00:15:55,951 --> 00:15:58,385
- What are you doing here?

347
00:15:58,385 --> 00:16:01,597
- I was looking for Captain Kemp.

348
00:16:01,597 --> 00:16:05,349
- This place is reserved
for space personnel only.

349
00:16:05,349 --> 00:16:06,349
- I'm sorry.

350
00:16:08,579 --> 00:16:09,681
Are you Captain Kemp?

351
00:16:09,681 --> 00:16:11,048
- Will you go away?

352
00:16:11,048 --> 00:16:11,947
- [Crow] Look at my stripes.

353
00:16:11,947 --> 00:16:13,288
Of course, I'm Captain Kemp.

354
00:16:13,288 --> 00:16:14,121
- No!

355
00:16:15,502 --> 00:16:17,322
- [Voiceover] Monorail
to Moon City will depart

356
00:16:17,322 --> 00:16:18,956
in seven minutes.

357
00:16:18,956 --> 00:16:21,150
(whistling)

358
00:16:21,150 --> 00:16:22,900
- [Tom] Oh, hi Gumby.

359
00:16:30,394 --> 00:16:32,394
- Otto, Otto was killed?

360
00:16:33,310 --> 00:16:35,227
- A couple of days ago.

361
00:16:36,854 --> 00:16:40,462
- [Tom] Yeah, we're dedicating
the locker room to him.

362
00:16:40,462 --> 00:16:42,920
- Retro engines failed on
landing, that's what I heard.

363
00:16:42,920 --> 00:16:45,003
Went straight in, crunch.

364
00:16:47,236 --> 00:16:48,398
- [Bill] They ought to
put that thing outside

365
00:16:48,398 --> 00:16:49,944
where people could read it.

366
00:16:49,944 --> 00:16:51,820
- Passengers wouldn't like it.

367
00:16:51,820 --> 00:16:53,401
It would worry them.

368
00:16:53,401 --> 00:16:58,362
- [Crow] Yeah, but to make
it splash guard for a urinal.

369
00:16:58,362 --> 00:17:00,445
- Did you get the 12,000?

370
00:17:06,114 --> 00:17:06,947
- [Joel] Here, I don't need it.

371
00:17:06,947 --> 00:17:07,986
I already have Pete Rose.

372
00:17:07,986 --> 00:17:11,672
- Hey, he flew the same sort
of space ferry you've got,

373
00:17:11,672 --> 00:17:12,505
didn't he?

374
00:17:12,505 --> 00:17:16,422
- Mm hmm, his was a little
bit older, that's all.

375
00:17:16,422 --> 00:17:20,514
- [Tom] A little bit wiser,
something to think about.

376
00:17:20,514 --> 00:17:22,847
(gun fires)

377
00:17:24,934 --> 00:17:27,946
- [Crow] They dressed their
stewardesses like Nazis.

378
00:17:27,946 --> 00:17:29,542
- We're leaving right away Mr. Hubbard.

379
00:17:29,542 --> 00:17:30,375
Magazine?

380
00:17:30,375 --> 00:17:31,208
- [Tom] Yes, it is.

381
00:17:31,208 --> 00:17:32,125
- Cocktail?

382
00:17:32,962 --> 00:17:33,795
Beer, sir?

383
00:17:33,795 --> 00:17:35,104
- He doesn't drink and he can't read.

384
00:17:35,104 --> 00:17:38,148
- [Joel] But he still has feelings, geez.

385
00:17:38,148 --> 00:17:39,395
They're so rich, they go to movies

386
00:17:39,395 --> 00:17:42,442
and they don't even watch 'em.

387
00:17:42,442 --> 00:17:43,275
- Janie.

388
00:17:43,275 --> 00:17:44,342
- Hello, Bill.

389
00:17:44,342 --> 00:17:45,862
- You look great.

390
00:17:45,862 --> 00:17:47,154
- [Crow] When'd you join the guard?

391
00:17:47,154 --> 00:17:49,211
Right after I turned in
the Von Trapp family.

392
00:17:49,211 --> 00:17:52,244
- They know me on the inside though.

393
00:17:52,244 --> 00:17:54,127
- Got a seat?

394
00:17:54,127 --> 00:17:56,710
- One up front for the Captain.

395
00:18:00,511 --> 00:18:03,492
This is Captain Kemp, Miss Taplin.

396
00:18:03,492 --> 00:18:04,415
- [Clem] You said.

397
00:18:04,415 --> 00:18:05,499
- I'm always at a disadvantage

398
00:18:05,499 --> 00:18:07,351
when I haven't got any clothes on.

399
00:18:07,351 --> 00:18:09,013
- I never noticed it.

400
00:18:09,013 --> 00:18:10,945
- Well, why did you wanna see me?

401
00:18:10,945 --> 00:18:12,998
- [Tom] You think I look like a zucchini?

402
00:18:12,998 --> 00:18:15,365
- [Crow] She looks like Darth Vader.

403
00:18:15,365 --> 00:18:17,949
- Told me you'd fly over
to Far Side, occasionally.

404
00:18:17,949 --> 00:18:18,840
- Mm hmm.

405
00:18:18,840 --> 00:18:21,338
- I wondered if you know my brother.

406
00:18:21,338 --> 00:18:22,171
- [Tom] Gary Larson.

407
00:18:22,171 --> 00:18:25,153
- Wally Taplin, he's a miner.

408
00:18:25,153 --> 00:18:27,167
- No, no I don't think I know him.

409
00:18:27,167 --> 00:18:29,217
- He was supposed to meet
me at the space port.

410
00:18:29,217 --> 00:18:31,177
- He's probably waiting at Moon City.

411
00:18:31,177 --> 00:18:32,533
- That's what the man said.

412
00:18:32,533 --> 00:18:35,129
- [Joel] Don't you listen
to what the man says.

413
00:18:35,129 --> 00:18:36,143
- Your first time up here?

414
00:18:36,143 --> 00:18:36,976
- Yes.

415
00:18:36,976 --> 00:18:38,409
- [Tom] That would explain the stupid hat.

416
00:18:38,409 --> 00:18:40,391
- I suggest you look up front.

417
00:18:40,391 --> 00:18:43,308
- [Tom] While I check out the rear.

418
00:18:47,316 --> 00:18:50,225
- [Clem] My God, it's so bleak.

419
00:18:50,225 --> 00:18:54,392
- [Bill] No air, no
vegetation, 1/6 gravity.

420
00:18:55,507 --> 00:18:56,340
- [Crow] Yeah, but on the good side,

421
00:18:56,340 --> 00:18:57,767
there's no Howie Mandell.

422
00:18:57,767 --> 00:18:59,517
- 14 days and nights.

423
00:19:00,489 --> 00:19:04,717
I suppose bleak is as good
a way as any to describe it.

424
00:19:04,717 --> 00:19:09,035
- [Joel] Yeah, and everybody
gets their own movie.

425
00:19:09,035 --> 00:19:10,446
- We always will be.

426
00:19:10,446 --> 00:19:12,466
- [Crow] Some are more foreign
than others, Velvet-head.

427
00:19:12,466 --> 00:19:15,549
- Perhaps, we should never have come.

428
00:19:21,487 --> 00:19:23,892
- [Tom] In the future,
bras will grow on the Moon.

429
00:19:23,892 --> 00:19:25,142
Cross my heart.

430
00:19:33,457 --> 00:19:35,385
- [Crow] Hey, it's the
original Space Mountain.

431
00:19:35,385 --> 00:19:36,369
What'd ya know?

432
00:19:36,369 --> 00:19:39,036
And look, the line's still long.

433
00:19:41,005 --> 00:19:42,867
- The monument there is
where Neil Armstrong landed

434
00:19:42,867 --> 00:19:43,784
by in 1969.

435
00:19:44,729 --> 00:19:46,079
- [Joel] Of course, that's just a model.

436
00:19:46,079 --> 00:19:48,579
The real thing's a lot bigger.

437
00:19:49,433 --> 00:19:50,829
- And the ice mines.

438
00:19:50,829 --> 00:19:51,896
- The what?

439
00:19:51,896 --> 00:19:52,896
- Ice mines.

440
00:19:55,088 --> 00:19:57,787
There are layers of ice a mile down,

441
00:19:57,787 --> 00:19:59,963
melt it into water and you drink it.

442
00:19:59,963 --> 00:20:02,748
Break it into oxygen, we breathe it.

443
00:20:02,748 --> 00:20:06,039
- [Crow] Crush it and flavor
it, you got a Slurpee.

444
00:20:06,039 --> 00:20:09,611
- [Tom] And D-cups as
far as the eye can see.

445
00:20:09,611 --> 00:20:12,613
- And when they die we eat them.

446
00:20:12,613 --> 00:20:16,471
There is nothing like it anywhere else.

447
00:20:16,471 --> 00:20:19,366
- It's funny to think that
we've no air out there.

448
00:20:19,366 --> 00:20:21,912
Nobody can just open up a window.

449
00:20:21,912 --> 00:20:22,745
- [Joel] Oh, we've done that,

450
00:20:22,745 --> 00:20:24,945
but you just have to be really fast.

451
00:20:24,945 --> 00:20:27,413
- Captain Kemp, I'm afraid
that we have no reservation

452
00:20:27,413 --> 00:20:30,131
in the name of Mr. Wallace Taplin,

453
00:20:30,131 --> 00:20:33,903
nor have we any messages for
a Miss Clementine Taplin.

454
00:20:33,903 --> 00:20:35,385
- [Crow] Oh, yeah, yeah, sure.

455
00:20:35,385 --> 00:20:37,475
Don't you have to go
make some Elfin cookies?

456
00:20:37,475 --> 00:20:39,269
(laughs)

457
00:20:39,269 --> 00:20:40,763
- [Tom] She looks like an Oompa Loompa.

458
00:20:40,763 --> 00:20:42,532
- Oh, it could be he's just late.

459
00:20:42,532 --> 00:20:44,679
The monorail doesn't go
any farther than Moon City,

460
00:20:44,679 --> 00:20:46,940
so he'd be coming by Bug.

461
00:20:46,940 --> 00:20:47,773
- Bug.

462
00:20:49,011 --> 00:20:51,070
- It's a way to get around up here.

463
00:20:51,070 --> 00:20:53,229
They run convoys of them over to Far Side.

464
00:20:53,229 --> 00:20:56,048
- [Tom] It's like a Moon base.

465
00:20:56,048 --> 00:20:57,463
- [Crow] Vroom, vroom.

466
00:20:57,463 --> 00:20:58,766
- Where's his claim.

467
00:20:58,766 --> 00:21:00,349
- Spectacle Crater.

468
00:21:05,537 --> 00:21:07,068
- [Crow] Well, the water's
turned off on the fountain,

469
00:21:07,068 --> 00:21:10,876
so I can show you where
he is here, right there.

470
00:21:10,876 --> 00:21:14,050
- The nearest base is Far Side 5.

471
00:21:14,050 --> 00:21:18,696
Now, that's at least
2,000 miles to Moon City.

472
00:21:18,696 --> 00:21:20,956
Six days by convoy.

473
00:21:20,956 --> 00:21:21,789
- That means it takes longer

474
00:21:21,789 --> 00:21:23,540
to get from one side of
the Moon to the other.

475
00:21:23,540 --> 00:21:25,579
- [Joel] Ah no, but thanks for playing.

476
00:21:25,579 --> 00:21:26,703
- That's right.

477
00:21:26,703 --> 00:21:28,327
- What about your spaceship?

478
00:21:28,327 --> 00:21:30,741
- Ferry, it's only a Moon ferry.

479
00:21:30,741 --> 00:21:32,001
- [Tom] At least he's open about it.

480
00:21:32,001 --> 00:21:34,322
- We'll have to do it in 20 minutes.

481
00:21:34,322 --> 00:21:37,210
- [Crow] That's because
this is the future.

482
00:21:37,210 --> 00:21:39,206
- But I can send a radio message, can't I?

483
00:21:39,206 --> 00:21:40,039
To see if he's left.

484
00:21:40,039 --> 00:21:40,914
- Oh, and no.

485
00:21:40,914 --> 00:21:42,805
Well, you could've last week.

486
00:21:42,805 --> 00:21:45,047
You see, we use a ring of
communication satellites

487
00:21:45,047 --> 00:21:46,892
for relaying waves around the curve,

488
00:21:46,892 --> 00:21:48,832
but one was hit by a meteorite.

489
00:21:48,832 --> 00:21:52,992
So, we're out of touch
with Far Side, right now.

490
00:21:52,992 --> 00:21:54,878
- The 21st century.

491
00:21:54,878 --> 00:21:57,429
- It'll still be the same in the 25th.

492
00:21:57,429 --> 00:21:58,981
- I suppose so.

493
00:21:58,981 --> 00:22:00,577
- [Joel] We're almost walking off the set.

494
00:22:00,577 --> 00:22:03,494
Would you mind going the other way?

495
00:22:04,522 --> 00:22:06,468
- Splurge out, buy yourself a new outfit.

496
00:22:06,468 --> 00:22:07,362
You'll feel better.

497
00:22:07,362 --> 00:22:09,072
- [Joel] Oh, we better
get out of here, too.

498
00:22:09,072 --> 00:22:11,380
We're walking off the set.

499
00:22:11,380 --> 00:22:12,797
- Can I help you?

500
00:22:14,607 --> 00:22:16,690
(thumps)

501
00:22:23,222 --> 00:22:26,852
- On July 22, 1969, a man first set foot

502
00:22:26,852 --> 00:22:28,734
on the surface of the Moon.

503
00:22:28,734 --> 00:22:31,901
His name, Vince Lombardi. (laughs)

504
00:22:31,901 --> 00:22:34,270
It's Neil Armstrong.

505
00:22:34,270 --> 00:22:35,925
This is our tribute to
astronaut Armstrong,

506
00:22:35,925 --> 00:22:38,260
and the men who brought him to the Moon.

507
00:22:38,260 --> 00:22:40,892
Crow will play the part of Neil Armstrong.

508
00:22:40,892 --> 00:22:42,780
- That's one small step for man,

509
00:22:42,780 --> 00:22:43,613
- Save it!

510
00:22:43,613 --> 00:22:44,503
- What? Oh.

511
00:22:44,503 --> 00:22:45,787
Sorry.

512
00:22:45,787 --> 00:22:48,371
- Joel will play the part of
the second man on the Moon,

513
00:22:48,371 --> 00:22:52,371
Edwin Buzz Aldrin and
President John F. Kennedy.

514
00:22:53,907 --> 00:22:55,401
I'll play the part of Mission Control

515
00:22:55,401 --> 00:22:59,751
and astronaut, Michael
Collins orbiting the Moon.

516
00:22:59,751 --> 00:23:00,880
- [Joel] Okay.

517
00:23:00,880 --> 00:23:03,703
- Ready? Okay, cue Joel.

518
00:23:03,703 --> 00:23:07,188
- [Joel] Ask not what your
country can do for you,

519
00:23:07,188 --> 00:23:10,960
rather what you can do for your country.

520
00:23:10,960 --> 00:23:14,455
- It was 1962, and a young
President, John F. Kennedy

521
00:23:14,455 --> 00:23:16,205
makes a bold promise.

522
00:23:17,413 --> 00:23:20,723
- By the end of this decade,
we will send a man safely

523
00:23:20,723 --> 00:23:23,973
to the Moon and return him safely home.

524
00:23:26,472 --> 00:23:29,882
- [Crow] Not bad, Joel, but
you know you sound a little bit

525
00:23:29,882 --> 00:23:32,395
like Cliff from Cheers.

526
00:23:32,395 --> 00:23:33,420
This is my big scene.

527
00:23:33,420 --> 00:23:35,670
- Not quite 10 years later.

528
00:23:38,407 --> 00:23:42,139
- [Joel] 40 feet, down 2
1/2, picking up some dust.

529
00:23:42,139 --> 00:23:46,056
30 seconds, contact light
okay, engine stopped.

530
00:23:47,414 --> 00:23:49,518
- [Tom] We copy you down, Eagle.

531
00:23:49,518 --> 00:23:51,510
- [Joel] Houston, Tranquility Base here.

532
00:23:51,510 --> 00:23:54,019
The Eagle has landed.

533
00:23:54,019 --> 00:23:55,759
- [Tom] Roger, Tranquility,
we copy you on the ground.

534
00:23:55,759 --> 00:23:57,925
You got a bunch of guys down
here about to turn blue.

535
00:23:57,925 --> 00:24:00,349
We're breathing again, thanks a lot.

536
00:24:00,349 --> 00:24:02,211
- [Joel] Thank you.

537
00:24:02,211 --> 00:24:03,331
- Now, now, okay.

538
00:24:03,331 --> 00:24:05,517
I'm on the pad now.

539
00:24:05,517 --> 00:24:06,850
I'm jumping off.

540
00:24:08,575 --> 00:24:12,742
That's one small step for man,
one giant leap for mankind.

541
00:24:16,597 --> 00:24:17,726
- [Joel] Okay, here I come.

542
00:24:17,726 --> 00:24:21,327
I'm coming down out of the Lunar Module.

543
00:24:21,327 --> 00:24:23,972
- Hey Joel, how come your
costume's not very good.

544
00:24:23,972 --> 00:24:26,357
- Well, I had to be the
President and Buzz Aldrin.

545
00:24:26,357 --> 00:24:27,357
Okay.
- Okay.

546
00:24:29,003 --> 00:24:29,911
- Oh man, did it.

547
00:24:29,911 --> 00:24:30,744
Do you have your keys?

548
00:24:30,744 --> 00:24:32,910
I just locked ourselves out of the LEM.

549
00:24:32,910 --> 00:24:33,847
- You what?

550
00:24:33,847 --> 00:24:36,976
Oh wow, that's one small screw-up for you,

551
00:24:36,976 --> 00:24:39,347
one giant screw-up for you.

552
00:24:39,347 --> 00:24:40,180
- I'm gonna have to find,--

553
00:24:40,180 --> 00:24:41,013
- Thus, ends our pageant.

554
00:24:41,013 --> 00:24:42,096
- that thing.

555
00:24:43,102 --> 00:24:46,935
("Moon Zero Two" theme music)

556
00:24:52,283 --> 00:24:53,257
- [Tom] What's that?

557
00:24:53,257 --> 00:24:54,098
- [Joel] I don't know.

558
00:24:54,098 --> 00:24:55,370
- [Tom] Whoa.

559
00:24:55,370 --> 00:24:57,466
(carefree dance music)

560
00:24:57,466 --> 00:24:58,502
- [Joel] Yowsa.

561
00:24:58,502 --> 00:25:01,341
- [Crow] There wearing Otello
game pieces on their head.

562
00:25:01,341 --> 00:25:02,758
- [Joel] Othello.

563
00:25:04,879 --> 00:25:08,258
You know, I kinda miss the morning, guys.

564
00:25:08,258 --> 00:25:09,670
- [Tom] I can see why.

565
00:25:09,670 --> 00:25:11,670
- [Joel] Hey, Crow, you can't wear that.

566
00:25:11,670 --> 00:25:14,253
- [Crow] Ow, ow, ow, thank you.

567
00:25:16,315 --> 00:25:17,466
Hey, it looks like Liza Minnelli

568
00:25:17,466 --> 00:25:20,299
and Lola Falana had a garage sale.

569
00:25:21,180 --> 00:25:22,603
- I miss him, too.

570
00:25:22,603 --> 00:25:25,018
He was a very good
customer and a good man.

571
00:25:25,018 --> 00:25:26,601
- And a good pilot.

572
00:25:27,563 --> 00:25:29,396
As pilots go, he went.

573
00:25:31,410 --> 00:25:33,796
- [Bill] Moon Flower, double.

574
00:25:33,796 --> 00:25:35,636
- [Tom] Ah, the name's
Bob if you don't mind.

575
00:25:35,636 --> 00:25:37,279
- It's an old fashioned Pampas Punch.

576
00:25:37,279 --> 00:25:39,676
- [Crow] Pamper's Punch? Ick.

577
00:25:39,676 --> 00:25:42,926
(carefree dance music)

578
00:25:45,338 --> 00:25:47,106
- First one's on the house.

579
00:25:47,106 --> 00:25:49,794
- [Tom] He didn't buy me a drink.

580
00:25:49,794 --> 00:25:52,530
- [Joel] Nice bar, really
crummy atmosphere, though.

581
00:25:52,530 --> 00:25:53,774
(laughs)

582
00:25:53,774 --> 00:25:55,018
- [Tom] That one will live forever.

583
00:25:55,018 --> 00:25:57,518
- [Crow] Lot of mileage there.

584
00:26:03,292 --> 00:26:05,779
- It still tastes like
distilled rocket fuel.

585
00:26:05,779 --> 00:26:08,279
- It is distilled rocket fuel.

586
00:26:09,503 --> 00:26:10,747
- [Crow] That's easy for you to say.

587
00:26:10,747 --> 00:26:12,326
- How much is cost this week?

588
00:26:12,326 --> 00:26:13,987
- $35 a shot.

589
00:26:13,987 --> 00:26:15,138
You see, we're a long way.--

590
00:26:15,138 --> 00:26:18,238
- A long way from Scotland, I know.

591
00:26:18,238 --> 00:26:19,269
- [Joel] Hey pal, I've got a small part.

592
00:26:19,269 --> 00:26:22,737
Let me finish my own
lines, if you don't mind.

593
00:26:22,737 --> 00:26:25,417
- But I haven't got 35 bucks a shot.

594
00:26:25,417 --> 00:26:27,999
- Isn't it about time you got hungry.

595
00:26:27,999 --> 00:26:30,249
- A good point, my Captain.

596
00:26:31,743 --> 00:26:35,445
And so, the grand space engineer passes

597
00:26:35,445 --> 00:26:39,713
into the great unknown
of Joe's old time.--

598
00:26:39,713 --> 00:26:42,044
- [Crow] Pizza Time Theater.

599
00:26:42,044 --> 00:26:44,294
- Perhaps, never to return.

600
00:26:45,928 --> 00:26:48,378
- He was Otto Von Beck's
engineer for a couple of years

601
00:26:48,378 --> 00:26:50,279
before he came to me.

602
00:26:50,279 --> 00:26:52,570
- Maybe he's just glad to be alive.

603
00:26:52,570 --> 00:26:54,878
- Sure, all engineers are crazy.

604
00:26:54,878 --> 00:26:57,766
- [Joel] Hey, that's a
pretty broad statement, pal.

605
00:26:57,766 --> 00:26:59,648
- What did you call this, Pampas Punch?

606
00:26:59,648 --> 00:27:01,518
- [Crow] No, it's bile. Why?

607
00:27:01,518 --> 00:27:04,178
- It tastes more like
Tijuana Brass Polish.

608
00:27:04,178 --> 00:27:06,522
- [Crow] Good one, ha ha ha!

609
00:27:06,522 --> 00:27:09,772
(carefree dance music)

610
00:27:15,360 --> 00:27:17,943
- [Joel] It's Moon Zero Mostel.

611
00:27:18,948 --> 00:27:21,448
You know what their answer is.

612
00:27:23,653 --> 00:27:25,629
- [Crow] Hairpieces by Alexander Calder.

613
00:27:25,629 --> 00:27:28,360
- [Joel] They're doin' the No Dance.

614
00:27:28,360 --> 00:27:29,943
No, no, no, no, no.

615
00:27:31,244 --> 00:27:33,411
(beeping)

616
00:27:37,151 --> 00:27:40,478
- [Tom] Hey, he could've just said hello.

617
00:27:40,478 --> 00:27:43,478
(fast-paced drum music)

618
00:27:47,070 --> 00:27:50,852
- [Crow] Hey, how are they
making that noise, Joel?

619
00:27:50,852 --> 00:27:53,845
- That's no way to treat a phone, friend.

620
00:27:53,845 --> 00:27:54,696
- I bought it a drink.

621
00:27:54,696 --> 00:27:56,025
What more can a man do?

622
00:27:56,025 --> 00:27:56,859
- [Joel] Well, maybe get it some dinner.

623
00:27:56,859 --> 00:27:59,152
- A gentleman wants a word with you.

624
00:27:59,152 --> 00:28:01,414
- You can tell him to go to hell instead.

625
00:28:01,414 --> 00:28:04,120
- Mr. Hubbard wants to see you.

626
00:28:04,120 --> 00:28:06,248
- Old 100% Hubbard?

627
00:28:06,248 --> 00:28:09,078
- [Crow] L. Ron Hubbard,
old Mother Hubbard?

628
00:28:09,078 --> 00:28:11,150
- He can go 100% to hell.

629
00:28:11,150 --> 00:28:13,154
- Let's just go and see Mr. Hubbard.

630
00:28:13,154 --> 00:28:14,992
- Convince me.

631
00:28:14,992 --> 00:28:16,117
- [Crow] I'm convinced.

632
00:28:16,117 --> 00:28:17,367
Oh, it's a gun.

633
00:28:19,428 --> 00:28:20,761
- I'm convinced.

634
00:28:22,095 --> 00:28:25,345
(carefree dance music)

635
00:28:33,389 --> 00:28:34,556
- Well, throw.

636
00:28:38,615 --> 00:28:40,543
- [Joel] Ooh, stupid.

637
00:28:40,543 --> 00:28:43,210
- [Tom] Let's Moon 'em, come on.

638
00:28:44,191 --> 00:28:45,645
- [Joel] Honey, fix your dress.

639
00:28:45,645 --> 00:28:47,145
- Community chest.

640
00:28:48,370 --> 00:28:49,367
- [Crow] Yeah.

641
00:28:49,367 --> 00:28:51,012
- Go to jail.

642
00:28:51,012 --> 00:28:52,845
Move directly to jail.

643
00:28:54,812 --> 00:28:57,812
Do not pass Go, do not collect $500.

644
00:29:02,240 --> 00:29:04,407
(sucking)

645
00:29:05,576 --> 00:29:07,043
- Will you stop sucking your thumb.

646
00:29:07,043 --> 00:29:08,210
- [Crow] Oops.

647
00:29:10,286 --> 00:29:12,266
- What are you going to do?

648
00:29:12,266 --> 00:29:13,722
- I'll go to jail.

649
00:29:13,722 --> 00:29:14,899
- Now, while you are in jail,

650
00:29:14,899 --> 00:29:18,814
I foreclose on the mortgage
and I bankrupt you.

651
00:29:18,814 --> 00:29:20,391
- [Joel] Neat, well, then I'm not going

652
00:29:20,391 --> 00:29:22,974
to be Community Chest any more.

653
00:29:24,843 --> 00:29:28,073
- It leaves you 19,000 short.

654
00:29:28,073 --> 00:29:29,001
You owe me 17 cents.

655
00:29:29,001 --> 00:29:30,821
- [Joel] Nice pocket calculator.

656
00:29:30,821 --> 00:29:32,645
- [Crow] Yeah, if you're a kangaroo.

657
00:29:32,645 --> 00:29:34,330
- And don't forget the interest.

658
00:29:34,330 --> 00:29:35,505
- [Tom] I'm not interested in interest.

659
00:29:35,505 --> 00:29:37,590
(ringing)

660
00:29:37,590 --> 00:29:40,590
- That will be Harry with our guest.

661
00:29:43,558 --> 00:29:44,860
- [Joel] Do you bathe?

662
00:29:44,860 --> 00:29:49,027
- [Tom] Fourth floor,
tyrannical tycoons, loose women.

663
00:29:51,136 --> 00:29:52,553
Works every time.

664
00:29:57,196 --> 00:29:58,696
Don't anyone talk.

665
00:30:05,225 --> 00:30:07,455
- Ah, it's Scotch.

666
00:30:07,455 --> 00:30:09,477
Now, why didn't you say so?

667
00:30:09,477 --> 00:30:11,468
- [Joel] Oh, I was
concentrating on my lines.

668
00:30:11,468 --> 00:30:13,281
- [Bill] Play with guns
on the surface floor.

669
00:30:13,281 --> 00:30:16,121
One shot and we'd all been
breathing empty space.

670
00:30:16,121 --> 00:30:18,560
- So, that's why you didn't
try to take it off me up there.

671
00:30:18,560 --> 00:30:20,100
- Well, there had to be
a reason, didn't there?

672
00:30:20,100 --> 00:30:20,985
- [Crow] Not necessarily.

673
00:30:20,985 --> 00:30:22,664
Sometimes, people take it for no reason.

674
00:30:22,664 --> 00:30:26,257
- Your reputation didn't
exaggerate Mr Kemp.

675
00:30:26,257 --> 00:30:28,286
Perhaps I should apologize
for sending a man

676
00:30:28,286 --> 00:30:30,019
like Harry to fetch the pilot.

677
00:30:30,019 --> 00:30:31,655
- [Joel] No, I'm just wild about Harry.

678
00:30:31,655 --> 00:30:33,995
- [Hubbard] Of the first man on Mars.

679
00:30:33,995 --> 00:30:36,162
- [Joel] Is that who I am?

680
00:30:37,181 --> 00:30:39,763
- Say when.

681
00:30:39,763 --> 00:30:41,456
- A long time ago.

682
00:30:41,456 --> 00:30:43,400
- [Hubbard] And now,
you're flying a 10-year-old

683
00:30:43,400 --> 00:30:45,823
space ferry and salvaging dead satellites

684
00:30:45,823 --> 00:30:47,696
to sell to the junkyards.

685
00:30:47,696 --> 00:30:51,013
- [Joel] Don't talk about Sarah that way.

686
00:30:51,013 --> 00:30:52,151
(mooing)

687
00:30:52,151 --> 00:30:53,951
- Will you stop fiddling with that thing?

688
00:30:53,951 --> 00:30:56,111
- But I've never been to Switzerland.

689
00:30:56,111 --> 00:30:59,203
- The only thing worth
seein' are the banks.

690
00:30:59,203 --> 00:31:00,286
- [Crow] Grr.

691
00:31:01,445 --> 00:31:03,835
- I think you knew Otto Von Beck.

692
00:31:03,835 --> 00:31:05,187
- [Crow] An auto on the
surface of the Moon.

693
00:31:05,187 --> 00:31:06,267
It'll never work.

694
00:31:06,267 --> 00:31:07,587
- And I believe you are
the only other pilot

695
00:31:07,587 --> 00:31:11,053
on the Moon with a ship for charter.

696
00:31:11,053 --> 00:31:15,073
Otto was going to do a little job for me.

697
00:31:15,073 --> 00:31:16,363
Now.

698
00:31:16,363 --> 00:31:17,196
- Now?

699
00:31:18,147 --> 00:31:19,039
- [Tom] Now?

700
00:31:19,039 --> 00:31:20,498
- Do you know asteroids, Mr. Kemp?

701
00:31:20,498 --> 00:31:21,532
- [Tom] Sure, I have Atari.

702
00:31:21,532 --> 00:31:23,480
- The remains of a planet which exploded

703
00:31:23,480 --> 00:31:25,072
but was never formed.

704
00:31:25,072 --> 00:31:26,986
Some of them, grains of dust.

705
00:31:26,986 --> 00:31:31,153
Some the size of small Moons
and everything in between.

706
00:31:32,266 --> 00:31:33,900
- [Tom] You're making a
spectacle out of yourself.

707
00:31:33,900 --> 00:31:36,060
(laughs)

708
00:31:36,060 --> 00:31:38,100
- [Joel] Doesn't he look
like the head of KAOS?

709
00:31:38,100 --> 00:31:41,086
- [Crow] Yeah, I was just thinking that.

710
00:31:41,086 --> 00:31:42,622
- [Joel] Bernie Kopell?

711
00:31:42,622 --> 00:31:45,292
- The orphans of the
solar system, Mr. Kemp.

712
00:31:45,292 --> 00:31:46,393
- [Joel] The guy from That Girl.

713
00:31:46,393 --> 00:31:48,889
- And you wanna become a father.

714
00:31:48,889 --> 00:31:50,473
- [Tom] Is that how it works?

715
00:31:50,473 --> 00:31:53,031
- [Bill] You can't set
up a mine on an asteroid.

716
00:31:53,031 --> 00:31:55,467
The cost of flying the
equipment up, the supplies,

717
00:31:55,467 --> 00:31:58,760
bringing the ore back, it never pays.

718
00:31:58,760 --> 00:32:00,927
- Not mine it, Mr. Kemp.

719
00:32:00,927 --> 00:32:03,027
Land it, on the Moon.

720
00:32:03,027 --> 00:32:03,860
- Land it?

721
00:32:04,887 --> 00:32:08,179
You mean crash it, and that's
against the law in a big way.

722
00:32:08,179 --> 00:32:10,132
- [Crow] He's right, just ask John Landis.

723
00:32:10,132 --> 00:32:13,715
- A young pilot might
drop it on Moon City.

724
00:32:15,559 --> 00:32:18,221
Well, we don't do things that way.

725
00:32:18,221 --> 00:32:19,503
You would have the results

726
00:32:19,503 --> 00:32:22,336
of two years research to help you.

727
00:32:23,171 --> 00:32:25,671
- [Joel] Good Lord, he's ugly.

728
00:32:26,973 --> 00:32:28,757
He's so immense.

729
00:32:28,757 --> 00:32:32,125
- So, what's so special
about one asteroid?

730
00:32:32,125 --> 00:32:33,289
- [Joel] It's worth bonus points.

731
00:32:33,289 --> 00:32:34,318
You go into the next screen.

732
00:32:34,318 --> 00:32:35,151
- You'll find out yourself
when you get there,

733
00:32:35,151 --> 00:32:37,651
so I may as well tell you now.

734
00:32:39,455 --> 00:32:40,288
Whitsun.

735
00:32:44,721 --> 00:32:47,907
- [Crow] I don't even know my name.

736
00:32:47,907 --> 00:32:50,172
- Too small to have
either a name or a number,

737
00:32:50,172 --> 00:32:52,821
the asteroid was first
photographed in 1998,

738
00:32:52,821 --> 00:32:54,921
when it happened to
pass close to the Earth.

739
00:32:54,921 --> 00:32:57,853
But it was never investigated or plotted.

740
00:32:57,853 --> 00:32:59,686
- Until two years ago.

741
00:33:00,935 --> 00:33:01,841
- This film was taken

742
00:33:01,841 --> 00:33:04,061
by Mr. Hubbard's astronomical division.

743
00:33:04,061 --> 00:33:05,447
- [Joel] I'm sorry, that's my gallstone.

744
00:33:05,447 --> 00:33:07,768
- Barely 60 feet by 30 by 30,

745
00:33:07,768 --> 00:33:10,956
estimated mass approximately 6,000 tons.

746
00:33:10,956 --> 00:33:12,839
- [Tom] Heavy.

747
00:33:12,839 --> 00:33:14,874
- [Crow] This is me playing Breakout.

748
00:33:14,874 --> 00:33:17,588
- Do you read spectral grams, Mr. Kemp?

749
00:33:17,588 --> 00:33:19,588
- Some of the easy ones.

750
00:33:20,837 --> 00:33:23,744
This part looks like aluminum.

751
00:33:23,744 --> 00:33:25,580
- That's very good.

752
00:33:25,580 --> 00:33:28,827
It's quite similar to aluminum.

753
00:33:28,827 --> 00:33:30,982
- [Joel] Is that Richard
Seals' stupider brother?

754
00:33:30,982 --> 00:33:32,409
- In the heart of an exploding planet

755
00:33:32,409 --> 00:33:35,242
hundreds of millions of years ago.

756
00:33:36,133 --> 00:33:37,784
What is the name again, Whitsun?

757
00:33:37,784 --> 00:33:41,052
- A ceramic crystalline form
of corundum aluminum oxide.

758
00:33:41,052 --> 00:33:43,635
- [Tom] Or something like that.

759
00:33:44,541 --> 00:33:46,291
- Sapphire, Mr. Kemp.

760
00:33:47,885 --> 00:33:49,478
- [Tom] I love Sapphire.

761
00:33:49,478 --> 00:33:50,697
- Sapphire.

762
00:33:50,697 --> 00:33:51,559
- [Tom] She's great.

763
00:33:51,559 --> 00:33:54,392
- 6,000 tons of gemstone sapphire.

764
00:33:56,639 --> 00:33:57,655
- [Crow] Okay.

765
00:33:57,655 --> 00:34:01,572
- That's what's so special
about this asteroid.

766
00:34:02,650 --> 00:34:07,210
- [Crow] Well, that's my
birthstone, I'll do it.

767
00:34:07,210 --> 00:34:08,806
- Well?

768
00:34:08,806 --> 00:34:11,483
- Sounds like a nice profitable idea,

769
00:34:11,483 --> 00:34:14,066
but it's still against the law.

770
00:34:16,268 --> 00:34:18,043
- [Joel] So's your forehead, though.

771
00:34:18,043 --> 00:34:19,155
- [Whitsun] We understand
that you're already

772
00:34:19,155 --> 00:34:22,045
in trouble with the law,
on safety regulations.

773
00:34:22,045 --> 00:34:22,928
- [Tom] Hm?
- [Crow] Hm?

774
00:34:22,928 --> 00:34:24,569
- The bureau wants to ground you.

775
00:34:24,569 --> 00:34:27,145
- [Hubbard] Oh, come now, Whitsun.

776
00:34:27,145 --> 00:34:29,129
That's no way to treat our guest.

777
00:34:29,129 --> 00:34:30,680
- [Crow] Yeah, he's too old for grounding.

778
00:34:30,680 --> 00:34:34,552
- Mr. Kemp must be entirely
free to make up his own mind.

779
00:34:34,552 --> 00:34:35,996
- [Joel] Which is great for him.

780
00:34:35,996 --> 00:34:38,932
- And just suppose I freely
make up my mind to come in.

781
00:34:38,932 --> 00:34:40,384
What do I get out of it?

782
00:34:40,384 --> 00:34:44,156
- [Tom] Don Pardo, show 'em what they win.

783
00:34:44,156 --> 00:34:47,030
- A brand new space ferry.

784
00:34:47,030 --> 00:34:47,863
- [Joel] Hello?

785
00:34:47,863 --> 00:34:49,282
- It could come in handy.

786
00:34:49,282 --> 00:34:51,782
- We'll have you one tomorrow.

787
00:34:52,892 --> 00:34:54,399
- But at that price, you
could get your own ship,

788
00:34:54,399 --> 00:34:56,714
get your own crew.

789
00:34:56,714 --> 00:34:58,297
- But not secretly.

790
00:34:59,224 --> 00:35:03,062
You see, someone like myself
starts to outfit an expedition

791
00:35:03,062 --> 00:35:05,836
into space, well, it
attracts questions, snoopers.

792
00:35:05,836 --> 00:35:07,769
- [Tom] Snoopers and blabbers.

793
00:35:07,769 --> 00:35:10,805
- Investigations from the Bureau.

794
00:35:10,805 --> 00:35:14,305
But you, well, you just pop off into space

795
00:35:15,396 --> 00:35:16,405
on one of your scavenging--

796
00:35:16,405 --> 00:35:17,238
- [Tom] Hey!

797
00:35:17,238 --> 00:35:19,667
- Flights, no questions asked.

798
00:35:19,667 --> 00:35:23,990
And a few days later,
an asteroid just happens

799
00:35:23,990 --> 00:35:27,451
to land on the Far Side of the Moon.

800
00:35:27,451 --> 00:35:30,328
- [Whitsun] And since
nobody's bothered to plot it,

801
00:35:30,328 --> 00:35:31,161
nobody can say.--

802
00:35:31,161 --> 00:35:33,434
- [Crow] Oh, thank you, Vidal Sassoon.

803
00:35:33,434 --> 00:35:37,272
I don't look good and you don't look good.

804
00:35:37,272 --> 00:35:41,105
("Moon Zero Two" theme music)

805
00:35:43,299 --> 00:35:47,308
- [Voiceover] PanAm Spaceway,
Bug Express flight 83

806
00:35:47,308 --> 00:35:48,276
will lift off.--

807
00:35:48,276 --> 00:35:51,953
- [Crow] Hey, her boots are eatin' her.

808
00:35:51,953 --> 00:35:54,361
- [Tom] That's a tribute
to Bronco Nagurski.

809
00:35:54,361 --> 00:35:56,927
- [Joel] She was in
Yards of Leather I think.

810
00:35:56,927 --> 00:35:59,805
- Us unsalaried workers
have to keep working.

811
00:35:59,805 --> 00:36:00,742
- [Crow] Ouch!

812
00:36:00,742 --> 00:36:03,225
- Looks like quite a
heavy load you have there.

813
00:36:03,225 --> 00:36:04,544
What is it?

814
00:36:04,544 --> 00:36:06,987
- Oh, some experimental
propulsion equipment

815
00:36:06,987 --> 00:36:09,570
I wanna protest in space.

816
00:36:09,570 --> 00:36:11,936
- [Tom] Sure, did you get
it from a honeyMoon catalog?

817
00:36:11,936 --> 00:36:15,110
- Section 47C, paragraph one.

818
00:36:15,110 --> 00:36:18,611
No information about commercial
or industrial secrets

819
00:36:18,611 --> 00:36:20,524
need be disclosed without authority

820
00:36:20,524 --> 00:36:23,080
of the Moon Code Warrant.

821
00:36:23,080 --> 00:36:24,952
- [Joel] But I'm paraphrasing, of course.

822
00:36:24,952 --> 00:36:26,700
- There's more than one way to skin a cat.

823
00:36:26,700 --> 00:36:27,564
- [Tom] Everyone says that,

824
00:36:27,564 --> 00:36:28,833
but no one ever tells me the other way.

825
00:36:28,833 --> 00:36:30,333
- Good luck, Bill.

826
00:36:32,902 --> 00:36:34,711
- You'll have to marry that girl, Bill

827
00:36:34,711 --> 00:36:35,775
- What do you mean, have to?

828
00:36:35,775 --> 00:36:39,091
- It might be better than
have her throw you in jail.

829
00:36:39,091 --> 00:36:40,670
- [Crow] Looks like
these guys got suited up

830
00:36:40,670 --> 00:36:42,384
for a Foosball game.

831
00:36:42,384 --> 00:36:44,177
- You'd better marry her anyway.

832
00:36:44,177 --> 00:36:45,186
- [Joel] You gotta marry her, Bill.

833
00:36:45,186 --> 00:36:47,389
She's got the wedding bell blues.

834
00:36:47,389 --> 00:36:49,556
- [Crow] Up, up, and away.

835
00:36:51,245 --> 00:36:54,578
- [Joel] I'll just throw this back here.

836
00:36:55,529 --> 00:36:57,739
- [Tom] Ah, I'm intrinsically evil,

837
00:36:57,739 --> 00:37:00,260
and I've got a new jogging
suit I'm showing off.

838
00:37:00,260 --> 00:37:03,339
- Lift off of Earth Express.

839
00:37:03,339 --> 00:37:07,422
We understand it's a side
no tourist should miss.

840
00:37:08,612 --> 00:37:10,342
When will you be ready to lift off?

841
00:37:10,342 --> 00:37:13,562
- Yes, this time, we
can pay the bill, okay?

842
00:37:13,562 --> 00:37:15,645
- [All] Okay, Mr. Fawlty.

843
00:37:17,306 --> 00:37:18,988
- The pump valve on number 2.

844
00:37:18,988 --> 00:37:20,405
We are ready now.

845
00:37:22,368 --> 00:37:23,201
Passengers?

846
00:37:23,201 --> 00:37:25,174
- He's got a computer in that briefcase.

847
00:37:25,174 --> 00:37:26,199
- [Joel] And he's not afraid to use it.

848
00:37:26,199 --> 00:37:27,032
- [Crow] Duck!

849
00:37:27,032 --> 00:37:27,865
- He can help with the heavy work.

850
00:37:27,865 --> 00:37:29,282
- I'll get order.

851
00:37:31,695 --> 00:37:33,520
- That's Mr. Karminski, isn't it?

852
00:37:33,520 --> 00:37:34,687
Your engineer.

853
00:37:35,829 --> 00:37:37,719
What nationality is he?

854
00:37:37,719 --> 00:37:40,182
- [Crow] He's from Barcelona.

855
00:37:40,182 --> 00:37:42,109
- It wasn't quite what I asked.

856
00:37:42,109 --> 00:37:44,171
- We're all foreigners up here.

857
00:37:44,171 --> 00:37:46,907
- [Joel] Some more than others.

858
00:37:46,907 --> 00:37:48,390
- Ready to go?

859
00:37:48,390 --> 00:37:50,269
Without the pistol, please.

860
00:37:50,269 --> 00:37:51,929
- You don't take this off me twice.

861
00:37:51,929 --> 00:37:52,804
- Then the trips off.

862
00:37:52,804 --> 00:37:53,899
If that thing goes off in the ship,

863
00:37:53,899 --> 00:37:57,428
we'd never get back to explain why.

864
00:37:57,428 --> 00:37:59,345
- Give it to me, Harry.

865
00:38:01,037 --> 00:38:02,922
- [Crow] But I feel
totally naked without it.

866
00:38:02,922 --> 00:38:04,839
It's my only accessory.

867
00:38:06,107 --> 00:38:09,061
- [Joel] Give him the
pearl earrings too, Harry.

868
00:38:09,061 --> 00:38:11,833
- Let's hope we all
have a profitable trip.

869
00:38:11,833 --> 00:38:14,608
- Let you know in about four days.

870
00:38:14,608 --> 00:38:16,271
- [Joel] Pow, Sluggo.

871
00:38:16,271 --> 00:38:18,599
- [Voiceover] Please
report to immigration.

872
00:38:18,599 --> 00:38:22,682
Passenger flight 320 Mars,
report to immigration.

873
00:38:24,641 --> 00:38:26,058
- [Joel] Shut up.

874
00:38:27,452 --> 00:38:28,285
- [Tom] That's the coffee.

875
00:38:28,285 --> 00:38:31,618
That should be ready in a minute or two.

876
00:38:33,854 --> 00:38:34,755
- Okay, we're pressurized.

877
00:38:34,755 --> 00:38:36,837
You can take your helmets off.

878
00:38:36,837 --> 00:38:38,253
- [Joel] Just kidding.

879
00:38:38,253 --> 00:38:40,217
- Whitsun, I want the main course

880
00:38:40,217 --> 00:38:43,138
exactly 90 seconds after blastoff.

881
00:38:43,138 --> 00:38:45,448
- [Crow] You are a hungry little guy.

882
00:38:45,448 --> 00:38:47,480
- Are we letting him navigate?

883
00:38:47,480 --> 00:38:50,320
- He's got a better computer than we have.

884
00:38:50,320 --> 00:38:51,650
- Can he use it?

885
00:38:51,650 --> 00:38:53,124
- [Bill] He figures
counting miles per hour

886
00:38:53,124 --> 00:38:57,076
isn't so different from
dollars per second.

887
00:38:57,076 --> 00:38:58,292
- As long as he remembers he can't get

888
00:38:58,292 --> 00:39:00,162
miles per hour on credit.

889
00:39:00,162 --> 00:39:02,337
- [Joel] Ha ha, I love your wry naive wit,

890
00:39:02,337 --> 00:39:03,920
my European friend.

891
00:39:05,013 --> 00:39:07,346
- Request takeoff clearance.

892
00:39:08,415 --> 00:39:11,498
Round trip, no landing away as usual.

893
00:39:13,084 --> 00:39:16,917
- [Crow] First class or
coach, smoking or non?

894
00:39:19,972 --> 00:39:22,052
- [Voiceover] Zero Two, you're clear.

895
00:39:22,052 --> 00:39:24,433
Mars Express due in 20 minutes.

896
00:39:24,433 --> 00:39:25,743
- [Tom] But are we clear?

897
00:39:25,743 --> 00:39:28,910
- Zero Two, I'll try to miss him, out.

898
00:39:30,201 --> 00:39:33,460
- [Crow] They really did a great
job of antiquing that ship.

899
00:39:33,460 --> 00:39:34,867
- [Joel] Slap in the road tape, dude.

900
00:39:34,867 --> 00:39:38,026
And get the JD from under the seat.

901
00:39:38,026 --> 00:39:40,969
("Moon Zero Two" theme music)

902
00:39:40,969 --> 00:39:44,469
- [Crow] Kate Melonhead, get off my foot.

903
00:39:49,121 --> 00:39:52,604
- [Joel] He travels with Roller
Derby queens, it's great.

904
00:39:52,604 --> 00:39:54,715
- [Tom] Ah, it's Esther Williams.

905
00:39:54,715 --> 00:39:56,072
She's in a fish bowl.

906
00:39:56,072 --> 00:39:58,285
- [Voiceover] Got that
course yet, Whitsun?

907
00:39:58,285 --> 00:40:00,121
- Relative to the
electric, I wanna a course

908
00:40:00,121 --> 00:40:03,017
of alpha three, five, one,
Peter one, seven, nine,

909
00:40:03,017 --> 00:40:04,100
speed 17,500.

910
00:40:05,441 --> 00:40:06,788
- [Crow] Then a sherry, medium dry.

911
00:40:06,788 --> 00:40:08,465
- It sounds as if he knows.

912
00:40:08,465 --> 00:40:11,196
- [Bill] It does, doesn't it?

913
00:40:11,196 --> 00:40:12,575
- Do you think he means.--

914
00:40:12,575 --> 00:40:14,702
- [Crow] Woop, woop, woop, woop.

915
00:40:14,702 --> 00:40:17,202
- Oh, more or less, I suppose.

916
00:40:19,179 --> 00:40:21,687
- I hope it's not too difficult for you.

917
00:40:21,687 --> 00:40:23,805
- Not at all, Mr. Whitsun.

918
00:40:23,805 --> 00:40:25,153
- Well, if you have it on bitograph,

919
00:40:25,153 --> 00:40:27,805
I hope I can make the
situation clear to you.

920
00:40:27,805 --> 00:40:29,305
- It's over there.

921
00:40:31,053 --> 00:40:33,957
Well, it's not new, but I think it works.

922
00:40:33,957 --> 00:40:38,124
- [Joel] Are we talking
about the same thing here?

923
00:40:40,365 --> 00:40:44,532
- [Tom] Would you turn off
Peter and the Wolf, please?

924
00:40:49,583 --> 00:40:52,962
- The Earth, the Moon revolving around it.

925
00:40:52,962 --> 00:40:55,804
Now this year, this week,
the asteroid is making

926
00:40:55,804 --> 00:40:59,971
it's closest approach to the
Moon since 1998 on this course.

927
00:41:00,868 --> 00:41:01,701
- [Crow] It's the closest approach

928
00:41:01,701 --> 00:41:04,868
since I grew my bushy-wushy sideburns.

929
00:41:05,923 --> 00:41:08,411
- Just here, 45 hours from now.

930
00:41:08,411 --> 00:41:10,165
- [Joel] The Importance of Being Earnest,

931
00:41:10,165 --> 00:41:11,987
that's where I saw him.

932
00:41:11,987 --> 00:41:13,346
- [Crow] A Gentleman Caller, actually.

933
00:41:13,346 --> 00:41:15,061
But these rockets, as I was saying.

934
00:41:15,061 --> 00:41:18,478
- The asteroid will change to this orbit.

935
00:41:19,319 --> 00:41:21,370
You will observe that its speed relative

936
00:41:21,370 --> 00:41:22,873
to the Moon is quite slow

937
00:41:22,873 --> 00:41:25,588
and that it is only visible from Far Side.

938
00:41:25,588 --> 00:41:28,497
The radar stations on
Near Side cannot see it.

939
00:41:28,497 --> 00:41:30,169
You appreciate the importance of this?

940
00:41:30,169 --> 00:41:33,015
- If they could, we'd be in
jail the moment we got back.

941
00:41:33,015 --> 00:41:34,035
- Exactly.

942
00:41:34,035 --> 00:41:35,185
- [Joel] Have you ever been in jail?

943
00:41:35,185 --> 00:41:37,091
You'd like it quite a lot.

944
00:41:37,091 --> 00:41:38,699
- The asteroid will have
come within 10,000 miles

945
00:41:38,699 --> 00:41:40,485
of the Moon, we should return to it,

946
00:41:40,485 --> 00:41:42,498
take new measurements, reset the engines,

947
00:41:42,498 --> 00:41:44,144
and fire them again.

948
00:41:44,144 --> 00:41:45,089
- [Joel] Am I boring you?

949
00:41:45,089 --> 00:41:47,578
- The asteroid lands on the Moon.

950
00:41:47,578 --> 00:41:50,087
- Crunch, I get the picture.

951
00:41:50,087 --> 00:41:52,462
- The convoy's in from Fire Flight five.

952
00:41:52,462 --> 00:41:53,295
- [Tom] Huh?

953
00:41:53,295 --> 00:41:57,295
- [Crow] Well, I'm glad
they know what she said.

954
00:41:58,690 --> 00:42:00,094
- [Tom] Hey, there's a sale of panties.

955
00:42:00,094 --> 00:42:02,309
- [Joel] Hide the garnishes, quick.

956
00:42:02,309 --> 00:42:06,013
All the knickknacks gotta be moved.

957
00:42:06,013 --> 00:42:09,263
(lively country music)

958
00:42:10,231 --> 00:42:11,657
- [Crow] They sure are a shiny bunch.

959
00:42:11,657 --> 00:42:13,727
- [Joel] Hey, it's Randolph Mantooth.

960
00:42:13,727 --> 00:42:14,612
- I'm sorry, I thought you were my.

961
00:42:14,612 --> 00:42:15,862
- Wrong number.

962
00:42:19,287 --> 00:42:20,846
- [Joel] Is that rayon they're wearing?

963
00:42:20,846 --> 00:42:22,166
- [Crow] Naugahyde.

964
00:42:22,166 --> 00:42:24,833
- [Joel] Hey, it's Hee Haw 1999.

965
00:42:28,646 --> 00:42:30,010
- [Crow] Pretty soon,
they'll get those big,

966
00:42:30,010 --> 00:42:32,418
foam weird number one hands.

967
00:42:32,418 --> 00:42:35,668
(lively country music)

968
00:42:38,792 --> 00:42:40,055
- [Crow] Hey, anybody know where a guy

969
00:42:40,055 --> 00:42:41,962
can get some Armor All around here?

970
00:42:41,962 --> 00:42:43,918
- [Joel] Hey, it's the same
dance with different hats,

971
00:42:43,918 --> 00:42:47,501
but it's a completely
different experience.

972
00:42:50,056 --> 00:42:51,451
- [Crow] Excuse me, ma'am.

973
00:42:51,451 --> 00:42:52,980
Someone didn't finish your haircut.

974
00:42:52,980 --> 00:42:54,563
Oh my, I'm in love.

975
00:42:55,967 --> 00:42:59,161
- Excuse me again, but do
you know Mr. Wallace Taplin?

976
00:42:59,161 --> 00:43:00,667
- Wally, yes, I know Wally.

977
00:43:00,667 --> 00:43:01,758
- [Tom] Sounds like the Benny Hill Show.

978
00:43:01,758 --> 00:43:03,500
- Not well enough, apparently.

979
00:43:03,500 --> 00:43:05,065
- You a friend of his?

980
00:43:05,065 --> 00:43:06,582
- No, I'm his sister.

981
00:43:06,582 --> 00:43:07,808
- [Man in Gray] That's a great relief.

982
00:43:07,808 --> 00:43:09,148
- Was he on this convoy?

983
00:43:09,148 --> 00:43:10,518
- Well, if he was, I didn't see him.

984
00:43:10,518 --> 00:43:11,945
- [Crow] Well, what was his handle?

985
00:43:11,945 --> 00:43:13,938
- [Man in Plaid] Would you
like to sit down, take a drink?

986
00:43:13,938 --> 00:43:15,115
- Yes, yes, join us.

987
00:43:15,115 --> 00:43:17,341
- Three more rocket fuels, please.

988
00:43:17,341 --> 00:43:18,574
- [Crow] And an air freshener.

989
00:43:18,574 --> 00:43:20,740
You know their rocket
fuel contains no ethanol.

990
00:43:20,740 --> 00:43:23,238
- I haven't seen Wally
in maybe four months.

991
00:43:23,238 --> 00:43:24,071
- [Joel] Hand check.

992
00:43:24,071 --> 00:43:25,691
- He sent me a cable to meet him here.

993
00:43:25,691 --> 00:43:26,565
- [Man in Gray] You're just up from Earth?

994
00:43:26,565 --> 00:43:27,398
- [Clem] Yeah.

995
00:43:27,398 --> 00:43:28,231
- You try radioing him?

996
00:43:28,231 --> 00:43:30,901
You can't, that satellite's out, isn't it?

997
00:43:30,901 --> 00:43:32,366
You'd think they'd have
decent communications

998
00:43:32,366 --> 00:43:33,713
here on the Moon.

999
00:43:33,713 --> 00:43:35,689
- No government ever spent one dollar

1000
00:43:35,689 --> 00:43:36,950
until they've lost five.

1001
00:43:36,950 --> 00:43:38,194
- Yeah, five for us.

1002
00:43:38,194 --> 00:43:39,531
