1224
00:53:04,559 --> 00:53:06,846
And nobody seems to have seen
him in nearly four months.

1225
00:53:06,846 --> 00:53:08,410
- Well, Far Side is a big place.

1226
00:53:08,410 --> 00:53:10,446
You just don't go visiting someone

1227
00:53:10,446 --> 00:53:12,606
when it's 100 miles of rough country,

1228
00:53:12,606 --> 00:53:14,954
and in a Moon Bug which.

1229
00:53:14,954 --> 00:53:16,494
- [Crow] Hey, Florence Henderson hairdo.

1230
00:53:16,494 --> 00:53:19,661
Looks like she did it with Wesson oil.

1231
00:53:22,466 --> 00:53:25,912
- Mr. Kemp, do you know
anything about Moon mining laws?

1232
00:53:25,912 --> 00:53:27,052
- A little.

1233
00:53:27,052 --> 00:53:29,086
- Can you explain the two-year rule?

1234
00:53:29,086 --> 00:53:30,347
- [Bill] Well, you gotta
claim for two years

1235
00:53:30,347 --> 00:53:32,334
and if you haven't found
anything at the end of that time,

1236
00:53:32,334 --> 00:53:33,297
you get thrown out.

1237
00:53:33,297 --> 00:53:34,611
Somebody else gets it.

1238
00:53:34,611 --> 00:53:36,289
There's quite a waiting list.

1239
00:53:36,289 --> 00:53:37,572
- Seems a bit unfair.

1240
00:53:37,572 --> 00:53:41,003
- Maybe, but the Moon costs a
lot of money to get started.

1241
00:53:41,003 --> 00:53:43,196
And they can't have
people just digging holes

1242
00:53:43,196 --> 00:53:44,566
and not finding anything.

1243
00:53:44,566 --> 00:53:45,682
- [Tom] Well, unless
they call them craters.

1244
00:53:45,682 --> 00:53:47,175
- How long has your brother had his claim?

1245
00:53:47,175 --> 00:53:49,990
- Two years and three days time.

1246
00:53:49,990 --> 00:53:51,512
- [Joel] Who's got the
deed on that forehead?

1247
00:53:51,512 --> 00:53:52,890
That's what I'd like to know.

1248
00:53:52,890 --> 00:53:54,670
- In his cable, that's why I'm up here.

1249
00:53:54,670 --> 00:53:55,928
- Well, you better tell
him to get over here

1250
00:53:55,928 --> 00:53:57,980
and prove it before he loses his claim.

1251
00:53:57,980 --> 00:53:58,883
- [Clem] By radio?

1252
00:53:58,883 --> 00:53:59,925
- [Bill] Oh no.

1253
00:53:59,925 --> 00:54:01,125
- [Crow] He's talking to a lady.

1254
00:54:01,125 --> 00:54:05,541
- But the convoys, no they
wouldn't be quick enough.

1255
00:54:05,541 --> 00:54:07,980
- Mr. Kemp, will you fly me over there?

1256
00:54:07,980 --> 00:54:09,601
- [Joel] I don't know, how fast do you go?

1257
00:54:09,601 --> 00:54:12,266
- I know I haven't got $10,000 on me now,

1258
00:54:12,266 --> 00:54:13,889
but if Wally's found something,

1259
00:54:13,889 --> 00:54:15,898
we'll pay you back as
soon as we're selling it.

1260
00:54:15,898 --> 00:54:17,298
- I couldn't land on your brother's claim.

1261
00:54:17,298 --> 00:54:18,355
- [Tom] That would hurt.

1262
00:54:18,355 --> 00:54:21,286
It's much too rough country.

1263
00:54:21,286 --> 00:54:22,813
But we could go to Far Side 5.

1264
00:54:22,813 --> 00:54:26,684
It's a 24 hour ride in a Bug from there.

1265
00:54:26,684 --> 00:54:28,607
You can owe it to me.

1266
00:54:28,607 --> 00:54:30,652
Hey, what the hell,
I'm nearly rich anyway.

1267
00:54:30,652 --> 00:54:32,190
Let's have another drink.

1268
00:54:32,190 --> 00:54:35,096
- [Joel] Well, can I
finish the one I've got?

1269
00:54:35,096 --> 00:54:37,970
- Glen, I'd like two more
of whatever they are.

1270
00:54:37,970 --> 00:54:39,843
- [Tom] Sewage bilge.

1271
00:54:39,843 --> 00:54:41,166
- You weren't thinking
of taking the young lady

1272
00:54:41,166 --> 00:54:43,666
for a ride, were you Mr. Kemp?

1273
00:54:44,997 --> 00:54:46,870
- I'm a pilot for hire.

1274
00:54:46,870 --> 00:54:50,147
- You're already hired, drop her.

1275
00:54:50,147 --> 00:54:51,525
- It'll only be a three day trip.

1276
00:54:51,525 --> 00:54:52,798
I'll be back in time.

1277
00:54:52,798 --> 00:54:54,973
- [Crow] That's what the skipper said.

1278
00:54:54,973 --> 00:54:56,403
- Drop her.

1279
00:54:56,403 --> 00:54:57,386
- [Joel] That would hurt her.

1280
00:54:57,386 --> 00:54:59,551
- You seem to have your hand on me.

1281
00:54:59,551 --> 00:55:00,487
- [Joel] I'm not a guy here,

1282
00:55:00,487 --> 00:55:04,404
but I'm pretty sure you've
got your hand on me.

1283
00:55:06,139 --> 00:55:06,972
(grunts)

1284
00:55:06,972 --> 00:55:09,972
(lively band music)

1285
00:55:16,176 --> 00:55:18,616
- [Tom] Hey, watch the
Slush Puppie machine.

1286
00:55:18,616 --> 00:55:22,783
- If we're gonna play, we're
gonna play by my rules.

1287
00:55:23,831 --> 00:55:27,998
- [Crow] Yeah, but nobody
knows what your rules are.

1288
00:55:29,708 --> 00:55:32,524
- [Tom] Always have your
hair cut by a machine.

1289
00:55:32,524 --> 00:55:34,621
- [Joel] And always
look like Dennis Hopper.

1290
00:55:34,621 --> 00:55:36,287
- [Tom] Ooh, he gave him a snuggie.

1291
00:55:36,287 --> 00:55:38,807
- [Crow] In space, no
one can hear a wedgie.

1292
00:55:38,807 --> 00:55:40,807
(discordant orchestral music)

1293
00:55:46,355 --> 00:55:47,855
Look out Big Bird.

1294
00:55:49,076 --> 00:55:50,493
Oh, magic screen.

1295
00:56:00,446 --> 00:56:04,029
- [Joel] Oh, he should've
seen that comin'.

1296
00:56:11,450 --> 00:56:13,783
- [Tom] Picked up the spare.

1297
00:56:14,687 --> 00:56:16,687
(discordant orchestral music)

1298
00:56:26,008 --> 00:56:28,258
- Touch down in 17 minutes.

1299
00:56:31,770 --> 00:56:33,642
- Be careful, we've just gone weightless.

1300
00:56:33,642 --> 00:56:37,307
- I know, just like the Moon Express.

1301
00:56:37,307 --> 00:56:41,274
Though, your ship is
different in other ways.

1302
00:56:41,274 --> 00:56:42,326
- [Joel] You mean, no toilet paper?

1303
00:56:42,326 --> 00:56:43,775
Yeah, sorry about that.

1304
00:56:43,775 --> 00:56:47,589
- You are in for a nice, slow
night drive in a Moon Bug.

1305
00:56:47,589 --> 00:56:49,256
Just beautiful, too.

1306
00:56:50,167 --> 00:56:52,327
I'll stay with the ship
and give her a sprinkling.

1307
00:56:52,327 --> 00:56:53,676
- And get some sleep.

1308
00:56:53,676 --> 00:56:56,593
- [Crow] And give her a sprinkling.

1309
00:56:59,141 --> 00:57:01,327
- Approaching re-entry point.

1310
00:57:01,327 --> 00:57:04,160
- [Tom] That's our cue, doll face.

1311
00:57:05,647 --> 00:57:07,903
("Moon Zero Two" theme music)

1312
00:57:07,903 --> 00:57:09,070
- [Crow] Cool.

1313
00:57:09,993 --> 00:57:14,385
("Moon Zero Two" theme music)

1314
00:57:14,385 --> 00:57:18,546
- [Tom] Hey, could you
turn down that stereo?

1315
00:57:18,546 --> 00:57:21,661
("Moon Zero Two" theme music)

1316
00:57:21,661 --> 00:57:23,451
- [Crow] The old west.

1317
00:57:23,451 --> 00:57:25,621
In space, no one can art direct.

1318
00:57:25,621 --> 00:57:27,457
- [Voiceover] I haven't
seen Wally here in oh,

1319
00:57:27,457 --> 00:57:30,480
must be nearly four months by now.

1320
00:57:30,480 --> 00:57:32,288
I've tried to radio him a couple of times,

1321
00:57:32,288 --> 00:57:35,862
but it's always chancy
in the mountain country.

1322
00:57:35,862 --> 00:57:37,950
- How about relaying him?

1323
00:57:37,950 --> 00:57:42,181
- Well, I could ask Nick
Hunter to ask Louie Grenia

1324
00:57:42,181 --> 00:57:44,944
to ask Tad Cottle to
pass on a message, but.

1325
00:57:44,944 --> 00:57:47,145
- [Joel] But they're
all dead, it'd be silly.

1326
00:57:47,145 --> 00:57:48,693
- Kidney trouble the last fortnight.

1327
00:57:48,693 --> 00:57:49,898
And I know that Nick went over

1328
00:57:49,898 --> 00:57:52,577
to Moon City on the last convoy.

1329
00:57:52,577 --> 00:57:53,410
On the other hand.

1330
00:57:53,410 --> 00:57:54,419
- [Tom] Do I need to know this?

1331
00:57:54,419 --> 00:57:57,903
- Might be able to contact Bill Worther.

1332
00:57:57,903 --> 00:58:01,817
Oh no, he's off radio for
the next couple of days.

1333
00:58:01,817 --> 00:58:04,481
Sorry, there doesn't seem to be any way.

1334
00:58:04,481 --> 00:58:05,435
- Well, when you didn't hear from him,

1335
00:58:05,435 --> 00:58:07,451
didn't you think of
declaring an emergency?

1336
00:58:07,451 --> 00:58:08,756
- [Joel] Do you think this permanent wave

1337
00:58:08,756 --> 00:58:10,781
is a good idea for my hair?

1338
00:58:10,781 --> 00:58:12,498
- [Crow] Looks like Big Boy.

1339
00:58:12,498 --> 00:58:14,895
- The satellite's only
been dead for 12 days.

1340
00:58:14,895 --> 00:58:19,445
- I know that, but nobody
dies slowly on the Moon.

1341
00:58:19,445 --> 00:58:20,612
You know that.

1342
00:58:22,140 --> 00:58:23,940
- Well, can I take a Bug, then?

1343
00:58:23,940 --> 00:58:25,569
- One's out.

1344
00:58:25,569 --> 00:58:27,206
- [Tom] I said bug light.

1345
00:58:27,206 --> 00:58:31,373
- And the other, I'd need
a thousand dollars deposit.

1346
00:58:34,020 --> 00:58:37,020
- [Crow] Oh, I have that in my shoe.

1347
00:58:38,455 --> 00:58:42,205
- [Tom] Your Odor Eater,
thank you very much.

1348
00:58:43,728 --> 00:58:45,566
- [Joel] I'm afraid you're
over your credit limit.

1349
00:58:45,566 --> 00:58:48,733
Do you have a Dr. Scholl's pad on you?

1350
00:58:51,182 --> 00:58:52,150
- Is it ready?

1351
00:58:52,150 --> 00:58:53,894
- Ready as she'll ever be.

1352
00:58:53,894 --> 00:58:56,213
I guess she'll get you there.

1353
00:58:56,213 --> 00:58:57,433
- And back?

1354
00:58:57,433 --> 00:58:58,933
- Sure do hope so.

1355
00:59:00,502 --> 00:59:02,384
- Climb aboard.

1356
00:59:02,384 --> 00:59:04,184
- [Joel] Oh man, she's cherry, man.

1357
00:59:04,184 --> 00:59:07,172
Sam put on some thrush glass packs, man.

1358
00:59:07,172 --> 00:59:11,339
He's got some twin headers
with a 44 barrel, it screams.

1359
00:59:13,103 --> 00:59:14,768
- [Crow] The van's pretty nice, too.

1360
00:59:14,768 --> 00:59:17,684
- Move over, unless you wanna drive.

1361
00:59:17,684 --> 00:59:21,851
- [Crow] Hey, how many girls
have you had in your Moon Bug?

1362
00:59:23,886 --> 00:59:28,053
- [Tom] Well, if you've gotta
go, you'd better do it now.

1363
00:59:29,996 --> 00:59:31,859
- [Joel] How do you like
my new Moon sounds tape.

1364
00:59:31,859 --> 00:59:33,192
I feel a blast.

1365
00:59:40,590 --> 00:59:41,423
- [Crow] Hey, I know.

1366
00:59:41,423 --> 00:59:42,886
Let's sing car songs.

1367
00:59:42,886 --> 00:59:44,699
You know, like the Wiener Man song, yeah.

1368
00:59:44,699 --> 00:59:48,213
♫ I know a weiner man,
he owns a hot dog stand ♫

1369
00:59:48,213 --> 00:59:50,630
sorry, how about another one?

1370
00:59:59,375 --> 01:00:01,894
- [Joel] And so, they
set out in the wiener car

1371
01:00:01,894 --> 01:00:04,561
in search of the giant kielbasa.

1372
01:00:07,813 --> 01:00:11,980
- [Tom] Oops, we're outta gas. (laughs)

1373
01:00:18,174 --> 01:00:21,424
- [Crow] Hey, there's Jacques Cousteau.

1374
01:00:24,768 --> 01:00:26,208
- Sorry, but this is a part of the Moon

1375
01:00:26,208 --> 01:00:27,675
that tourists don't usually see.

1376
01:00:27,675 --> 01:00:29,331
If we pull the wrong lever out here,

1377
01:00:29,331 --> 01:00:31,307
there's no guarantee we'll even be found.

1378
01:00:31,307 --> 01:00:32,307
- I'm sorry.

1379
01:00:33,368 --> 01:00:35,195
But I'm not a tourist, Mr. Kemp.

1380
01:00:35,195 --> 01:00:37,283
I'm here to stay and work with my brother.

1381
01:00:37,283 --> 01:00:38,562
- [Joel] I know it's a
little bit rough now,

1382
01:00:38,562 --> 01:00:40,089
but it'll be better when we fall in love

1383
01:00:40,089 --> 01:00:42,198
later in the picture.

1384
01:00:42,198 --> 01:00:43,448
- I understand.

1385
01:00:45,417 --> 01:00:46,828
How long does it stay dark?

1386
01:00:46,828 --> 01:00:48,539
- [Joel] All night.

1387
01:00:48,539 --> 01:00:50,206
- Sunup in 40 hours.

1388
01:00:52,670 --> 01:00:55,569
- [Crow] So, that's like 100 hours.

1389
01:00:55,569 --> 01:00:58,253
- [Bill] 200 degrees below zero,

1390
01:00:58,253 --> 01:01:01,569
and up above boiling
point when the suns out.

1391
01:01:01,569 --> 01:01:03,162
Don't worry, these Bugs
have efficient heating

1392
01:01:03,162 --> 01:01:04,953
and cooling systems.

1393
01:01:04,953 --> 01:01:06,742
We've always got the Moon suits.

1394
01:01:06,742 --> 01:01:08,076
- [Joel] Yeah, I guess
if I had to do it over,

1395
01:01:08,076 --> 01:01:10,497
I'd probably buy a foreign.

1396
01:01:10,497 --> 01:01:12,330
- I see what you mean about

1397
01:01:12,330 --> 01:01:14,096
we'll always be foreigners.

1398
01:01:14,096 --> 01:01:17,096
- [Joel] No, I said I'd buy foreign.

1399
01:01:18,581 --> 01:01:20,576
- Why'd your brother pick
this end of the Moon.

1400
01:01:20,576 --> 01:01:21,838
- He didn't, it was the only claim left

1401
01:01:21,838 --> 01:01:23,170
when he got here.

1402
01:01:23,170 --> 01:01:24,511
- [Bill] That figures.

1403
01:01:24,511 --> 01:01:26,295
What was he mining on Earth?

1404
01:01:26,295 --> 01:01:28,066
- Gold, some silver.

1405
01:01:28,066 --> 01:01:31,033
Last thing he found was
copper up in Montana.

1406
01:01:31,033 --> 01:01:34,216
Sold out cheap to a big company.

1407
01:01:34,216 --> 01:01:35,531
He always thinks there'll be a bigger

1408
01:01:35,531 --> 01:01:37,953
and better strike over the next hill.

1409
01:01:37,953 --> 01:01:40,047
- He came over a big hill this time.

1410
01:01:40,047 --> 01:01:40,880
- [Joel] Funny.

1411
01:01:40,880 --> 01:01:41,713
- Your father?

1412
01:01:41,713 --> 01:01:43,852
- He blew himself up five years ago,

1413
01:01:43,852 --> 01:01:45,868
dynamiting for emeralds in the Andes.

1414
01:01:45,868 --> 01:01:46,965
- [Tom] And your mom?

1415
01:01:46,965 --> 01:01:48,532
- I hardly knew him.

1416
01:01:48,532 --> 01:01:50,087
He was always off somewhere,

1417
01:01:50,087 --> 01:01:53,356
digging a new hole over a new hill.

1418
01:01:53,356 --> 01:01:55,937
You're the Bill Kemp who
was the first man on Mars.

1419
01:01:55,937 --> 01:01:57,190
- That's right.

1420
01:01:57,190 --> 01:01:59,861
- It's funny, meeting you like this.

1421
01:01:59,861 --> 01:02:01,930
- Driving a hired Moon Bug?

1422
01:02:01,930 --> 01:02:04,461
- No, I can remember when we got the news

1423
01:02:04,461 --> 01:02:06,215
that you'd landed on Mars.

1424
01:02:06,215 --> 01:02:08,004
I was just a school girl then,

1425
01:02:08,004 --> 01:02:10,114
and I wasn't very
interested in space travel.

1426
01:02:10,114 --> 01:02:13,744
- [Crow] Okay, okay, rub it in, I'm old.

1427
01:02:13,744 --> 01:02:16,546
- Then the astronomy teacher
showed us Mars one night,

1428
01:02:16,546 --> 01:02:18,058
and told us how far it was.

1429
01:02:18,058 --> 01:02:19,225
- It still is.

1430
01:02:20,876 --> 01:02:23,036
- I remember your name from then.

1431
01:02:23,036 --> 01:02:26,526
The man who'd flown his
spaceship for 40,000,000 miles.

1432
01:02:26,526 --> 01:02:28,193
- [Crow] Okay, you're 12, I'm 100.

1433
01:02:28,193 --> 01:02:29,633
I get it already.

1434
01:02:29,633 --> 01:02:32,772
- Oh, I can still recite
you the names of the crew.

1435
01:02:32,772 --> 01:02:34,709
- That's more than I could.

1436
01:02:34,709 --> 01:02:35,542
- [Joel] Do you ever think about renting

1437
01:02:35,542 --> 01:02:37,524
out your forehead for advertising.

1438
01:02:37,524 --> 01:02:39,983
- Why'd you give it up, exploring?

1439
01:02:39,983 --> 01:02:41,595
- [Bill] It gave me up.

1440
01:02:41,595 --> 01:02:44,707
After Mars, and Jack Harvey
got on Venus, the corporation

1441
01:02:44,707 --> 01:02:48,911
decided that passengers
was where the money was.

1442
01:02:48,911 --> 01:02:50,155
Sure, it would've taken some money

1443
01:02:50,155 --> 01:02:53,689
and some new inventions
too, to get to Mercury,

1444
01:02:53,689 --> 01:02:55,356
and Jupiter's Moons,

1445
01:02:57,481 --> 01:02:58,332
but somebody's gotta do it.

1446
01:02:58,332 --> 01:03:00,931
- [Joel] Jupiter, that's
a planet, isn't it?

1447
01:03:00,931 --> 01:03:04,810
- Anyway, I wasn't a
passenger pilot, so I quit.

1448
01:03:04,810 --> 01:03:05,690
- [Crow] Okay, I lied.

1449
01:03:05,690 --> 01:03:07,689
They got me on a morals charge.

1450
01:03:07,689 --> 01:03:08,914
- I understand.

1451
01:03:08,914 --> 01:03:09,917
- You do?

1452
01:03:09,917 --> 01:03:11,951
- [Tom] Chinese good for you?

1453
01:03:11,951 --> 01:03:13,698
- [Crow] Hey, it's a souvenir bank.

1454
01:03:13,698 --> 01:03:16,472
- There's always something
over the next hill.

1455
01:03:16,472 --> 01:03:17,551
I know.

1456
01:03:17,551 --> 01:03:20,775
- And always on borrowed money.

1457
01:03:20,775 --> 01:03:24,032
But there is something over the hill.

1458
01:03:24,032 --> 01:03:25,916
- That's the trouble for women.

1459
01:03:25,916 --> 01:03:27,841
All space travel is just a big way

1460
01:03:27,841 --> 01:03:30,063
of getting out of the house
on a Saturday evening.

1461
01:03:30,063 --> 01:03:31,899
- Well, if you don't have
anything better to do.

1462
01:03:31,899 --> 01:03:34,149
- [Joel] And we sure don't.

1463
01:03:35,931 --> 01:03:39,764
("Moon Zero Two" theme music)

1464
01:03:44,951 --> 01:03:46,534
♫ I wanna be his wiener wife

1465
01:03:46,534 --> 01:03:48,721
♫ I wanna join his wiener life ♫

1466
01:03:48,721 --> 01:03:50,297
- [Crow] Hey, nice steps.

1467
01:03:50,297 --> 01:03:54,084
Looks like they should have
boxing gloves at the end of 'em.

1468
01:03:54,084 --> 01:03:55,114
- [Tom] Looks like they're kinda crawling

1469
01:03:55,114 --> 01:03:57,531
out of the Tupperware orange.

1470
01:04:02,303 --> 01:04:04,455
- [Joel] Now, those suits
are different colors,

1471
01:04:04,455 --> 01:04:06,622
so you can tell who's who.

1472
01:04:08,369 --> 01:04:09,620
If she gets confused,

1473
01:04:09,620 --> 01:04:13,290
she should just remember that she's her.

1474
01:04:13,290 --> 01:04:14,373
- [Crow] Huh?

1475
01:04:20,790 --> 01:04:22,499
- [Tom] Well, they left
their dome light on,

1476
01:04:22,499 --> 01:04:26,166
so someone's gonna have
to get a jump start.

1477
01:04:27,215 --> 01:04:29,349
AAA's even gonna charge them for that run.

1478
01:04:29,349 --> 01:04:31,420
- [Joel] Oh, brother.

1479
01:04:31,420 --> 01:04:34,420
Her brother lived in a port-a-potty.

1480
01:04:39,724 --> 01:04:40,715
- [Crow] Wow, look at it in there.

1481
01:04:40,715 --> 01:04:43,415
Your brother was really a slob.

1482
01:04:43,415 --> 01:04:44,719
Hey, is there air coming out of your suit,

1483
01:04:44,719 --> 01:04:47,136
or are you playing a trumpet?

1484
01:04:49,732 --> 01:04:51,734
- [Joel] Skin sample.

1485
01:04:51,734 --> 01:04:52,984
Looks valuable.

1486
01:04:55,499 --> 01:04:56,905
- [Tom] Wish I had me one of them plows.

1487
01:04:56,905 --> 01:04:59,538
- [Clem] I found something.

1488
01:04:59,538 --> 01:05:00,980
- [Tom] Their driveways.

1489
01:05:00,980 --> 01:05:01,813
- Where are you?

1490
01:05:01,813 --> 01:05:03,480
- Around the corner.

1491
01:05:04,994 --> 01:05:06,254
- Don't wander off.

1492
01:05:06,254 --> 01:05:08,594
You've gotta stay in sight.

1493
01:05:08,594 --> 01:05:10,095
- I'm sorry.

1494
01:05:10,095 --> 01:05:10,928
- [Tom] A setup like that is bound

1495
01:05:10,928 --> 01:05:12,472
to scare off trick-or-treaters.

1496
01:05:12,472 --> 01:05:14,125
- [Clem] Do you know what this is?

1497
01:05:14,125 --> 01:05:15,038
- A piece of rock.

1498
01:05:15,038 --> 01:05:16,233
- [Crow] Looks like
your brother's kneecap.

1499
01:05:16,233 --> 01:05:19,768
- There's a vein of nickel
in it, and a very rich one.

1500
01:05:19,768 --> 01:05:21,803
Wally did find something.

1501
01:05:21,803 --> 01:05:23,027
- Are you sure?

1502
01:05:23,027 --> 01:05:24,647
- I know a little about geology.

1503
01:05:24,647 --> 01:05:26,195
- [Tom] Oh, we all know a little bit.

1504
01:05:26,195 --> 01:05:27,680
- Where could he have gone?

1505
01:05:27,680 --> 01:05:32,124
- Not far, or he would
have taken his Bug Dozer.

1506
01:05:32,124 --> 01:05:33,926
- [Joel] Now, just how
disappointed would you be

1507
01:05:33,926 --> 01:05:37,426
if you never saw your brother alive again?

1508
01:05:40,121 --> 01:05:42,621
- Look, there's someone there.

1509
01:05:43,513 --> 01:05:45,564
- [Crow] Oh him, he's always there.

1510
01:05:45,564 --> 01:05:47,956
It's a space lawn jockey.

1511
01:05:47,956 --> 01:05:49,990
Everyone's got 'em at the
end of their driveway.

1512
01:05:49,990 --> 01:05:52,086
- [Joel] Now remember,
there'll be three of us now

1513
01:05:52,086 --> 01:05:56,086
and you're the one that's
you in the blue, okay.

1514
01:05:59,675 --> 01:06:00,508
(screeching)

1515
01:06:00,508 --> 01:06:01,421
- [Tom] Oh, he's lost weight.

1516
01:06:01,421 --> 01:06:02,883
He looked much better heavy.

1517
01:06:02,883 --> 01:06:05,050
(screams)

1518
01:06:10,418 --> 01:06:12,820
- [Clem] I thought he just made a mistake.

1519
01:06:12,820 --> 01:06:15,160
- [Bill] He managed for two
years without making one,

1520
01:06:15,160 --> 01:06:16,934
and he didn't puncture his suit.

1521
01:06:16,934 --> 01:06:21,594
He had to have oxygen to
look like, to go as he did.

1522
01:06:21,594 --> 01:06:23,261
One full, one empty.

1523
01:06:24,563 --> 01:06:27,352
- [Crow] Which one do you think he used?

1524
01:06:27,352 --> 01:06:30,045
- Just a stupid mistake.

1525
01:06:30,045 --> 01:06:32,447
- [Tom] Well, I bet
he'll never do it again.

1526
01:06:32,447 --> 01:06:36,614
- [Joel] Let's use our
precious oxygen and bury him.

1527
01:06:39,297 --> 01:06:41,366
- [Crow] See this, it's his Nintendo.

1528
01:06:41,366 --> 01:06:42,878
He would've wanted me to have it.

1529
01:06:42,878 --> 01:06:45,211
Well, if he'd have known me.

1530
01:06:47,486 --> 01:06:48,736
I'm keeping it.

1531
01:06:53,866 --> 01:06:56,533
- [Tom] Honey, cookies are done.

1532
01:06:58,932 --> 01:06:59,765
- Hold it.

1533
01:07:01,056 --> 01:07:03,584
- [Joel] Oops, looks like we got company.

1534
01:07:03,584 --> 01:07:05,667
It's the unwelcome wagon.

1535
01:07:09,322 --> 01:07:12,595
- You out there, identify yourselves.

1536
01:07:12,595 --> 01:07:13,953
- [Crow] Okay, will this help?

1537
01:07:13,953 --> 01:07:15,017
They call me Shooter.

1538
01:07:15,017 --> 01:07:17,100
(laughs)

1539
01:07:23,816 --> 01:07:25,790
- [Tom] Damn Moon goons.

1540
01:07:25,790 --> 01:07:27,790
(suspenseful orchestral music)

1541
01:07:38,570 --> 01:07:41,535
- [Crow] Hey, they come
in great flavors, too.

1542
01:07:41,535 --> 01:07:43,813
There's raspberry red, burnt umber,

1543
01:07:43,813 --> 01:07:45,980
and of course, chartreuse.

1544
01:07:46,935 --> 01:07:48,889
- [Joel] Oh man, and I
bet that Moon Buggy was

1545
01:07:48,889 --> 01:07:50,222
on his car, too.

1546
01:07:51,750 --> 01:07:53,750
(suspenseful orchestral music)

1547
01:08:01,864 --> 01:08:04,839
- [Tom] Excuse me, I'm nervous.

1548
01:08:04,839 --> 01:08:07,839
I'm sorry, you'll have to excuse me.

1549
01:08:09,341 --> 01:08:11,341
(suspenseful orchestral music)

1550
01:08:16,748 --> 01:08:17,801
- We're on telephone now.

1551
01:08:17,801 --> 01:08:19,086
They can't hear us.

1552
01:08:19,086 --> 01:08:20,482
But don't say anything on radio.

1553
01:08:20,482 --> 01:08:21,747
They're picking it all up.

1554
01:08:21,747 --> 01:08:23,125
- [Crow] Is there a seven second delay?

1555
01:08:23,125 --> 01:08:24,125
Can I swear?

1556
01:08:26,940 --> 01:08:28,377
- Come on out, both of you.

1557
01:08:28,377 --> 01:08:30,108
Or we'll drill you like a sieve.

1558
01:08:30,108 --> 01:08:30,941
- [Joel] What kinda sieve?

1559
01:08:30,941 --> 01:08:32,412
That oughta keep them busy.

1560
01:08:32,412 --> 01:08:33,562
- Don't shoot.

1561
01:08:33,562 --> 01:08:36,910
The girl's hurt, you've gotta help me.

1562
01:08:36,910 --> 01:08:39,293
- [Crow] Does this help?

1563
01:08:39,293 --> 01:08:41,710
(explosions)

1564
01:08:42,938 --> 01:08:44,056
- [Tom] Timpani guns.

1565
01:08:44,056 --> 01:08:46,441
(suspenseful orchestral music)

1566
01:08:46,441 --> 01:08:48,608
- [Joel] Works every time.

1567
01:08:50,026 --> 01:08:51,817
- [Crow] Well, you got lime
green, just raspberry red

1568
01:08:51,817 --> 01:08:53,734
and lemon yellow to go.

1569
01:08:54,634 --> 01:08:55,467
But remember, Trix are for kids.

1570
01:08:55,467 --> 01:08:57,467
(suspenseful guitar music)

1571
01:09:03,094 --> 01:09:05,577
- [Joel] Now, just remember,
they're the guys in color.

1572
01:09:05,577 --> 01:09:07,577
(suspenseful guitar music)

1573
01:09:15,214 --> 01:09:17,381
- There's another way out.

1574
01:09:19,509 --> 01:09:21,893
- [Crow] Hey, there's a barracuda in here.

1575
01:09:21,893 --> 01:09:22,784
- [Joel] If we don't die,

1576
01:09:22,784 --> 01:09:24,228
you wanna catch a movie or something?

1577
01:09:24,228 --> 01:09:26,244
I mean, if you're not
alive, I totally understand.

1578
01:09:26,244 --> 01:09:29,411
It won't hurt my feelings or anything.

1579
01:09:37,912 --> 01:09:38,745
- Back in the cave.

1580
01:09:38,745 --> 01:09:40,745
(suspenseful guitar music)

1581
01:09:45,394 --> 01:09:46,788
- [Crow] Hey, plow your driveway, mister?

1582
01:09:46,788 --> 01:09:47,621
Geez.

1583
01:09:50,094 --> 01:09:51,749
(screeching)

1584
01:09:51,749 --> 01:09:53,854
- [Tom] Aw, he killed a blow-up doll.

1585
01:09:53,854 --> 01:09:55,937
Soups on, he said flatly.

1586
01:09:57,279 --> 01:09:59,279
(suspenseful guitar music)

1587
01:10:05,099 --> 01:10:09,099
- [Joel] I think he's
going after a crack house.

1588
01:10:10,387 --> 01:10:12,367
- [Crow] Wow, lawn and garden attachments,

1589
01:10:12,367 --> 01:10:14,784
that guy's got all the candy.

1590
01:10:17,054 --> 01:10:21,461
- [Joel] Ah, just take a
little off the torso, please.

1591
01:10:21,461 --> 01:10:23,461
(suspenseful guitar music)

1592
01:10:39,901 --> 01:10:43,318
- [Joel] Seems like a bad music festival.

1593
01:10:46,390 --> 01:10:47,317
- [Crow] Hey, shut the door.

1594
01:10:47,317 --> 01:10:49,246
What are you born in a vacuum?

1595
01:10:49,246 --> 01:10:53,163
- [Tom] Hey, slippery
guy, he's playing possum.

1596
01:10:56,156 --> 01:10:57,060
(gun fires)

1597
01:10:57,060 --> 01:10:57,893
Told ya.

1598
01:10:58,734 --> 01:10:59,842
(gasping)

1599
01:10:59,842 --> 01:11:01,009
- [Crow] Oops.

1600
01:11:02,739 --> 01:11:05,322
- [Tom] Hey, hurt little fella?

1601
01:11:06,896 --> 01:11:08,696
- Quickly, give me your brother's pack.

1602
01:11:08,696 --> 01:11:10,128
- [Joel] How do I know it's you?

1603
01:11:10,128 --> 01:11:13,295
- [Crow] I'm wearing white, you idiot.

1604
01:11:16,591 --> 01:11:18,038
- [Joel] What good is it
if they're on the Moon,

1605
01:11:18,038 --> 01:11:22,502
if they just re-enact the
atrocities from Earth.

1606
01:11:22,502 --> 01:11:24,669
(gasping)

1607
01:11:25,879 --> 01:11:29,046
- [Tom] That's a good seal impression.

1608
01:11:30,347 --> 01:11:33,764
(eerie orchestral music)

1609
01:11:44,298 --> 01:11:46,998
- [Joel] This one's
going back to the store.

1610
01:11:46,998 --> 01:11:50,263
- [Tom] I think I may have just
put on the waste container.

1611
01:11:50,263 --> 01:11:53,283
- Whatever's in here, it isn't air.

1612
01:11:53,283 --> 01:11:54,171
- How do we get out of here?

1613
01:11:54,171 --> 01:11:56,004
Our Bug's written off.

1614
01:11:58,132 --> 01:12:00,057
- Let's try this one.

1615
01:12:00,057 --> 01:12:02,145
- [Crow] Yeah, it's got lawn attachments

1616
01:12:02,145 --> 01:12:06,062
and a Blaupunkt stereo,
factory air, the works.

1617
01:12:14,215 --> 01:12:16,636
- [Joel] Oh man, it smells like
a mushroom factory in here.

1618
01:12:16,636 --> 01:12:18,969
Those guys were really evil.

1619
01:12:20,227 --> 01:12:21,621
- Leave your suit on.

1620
01:12:21,621 --> 01:12:23,737
It's 200 degrees below zero in here.

1621
01:12:23,737 --> 01:12:25,087
- [Tom] What's the wind chill, though?

1622
01:12:25,087 --> 01:12:27,670
- Can't we turn the heating on?

1623
01:12:32,685 --> 01:12:34,384
- Oh, it's all shot to hell.

1624
01:12:34,384 --> 01:12:35,751
- [Crow] What, the plot?

1625
01:12:35,751 --> 01:12:37,624
- And we've only got
power for about 150 miles,

1626
01:12:37,624 --> 01:12:40,432
but not for heating and cooling, as well.

1627
01:12:40,432 --> 01:12:42,537
- We'll have to stay in
our suits all the way.

1628
01:12:42,537 --> 01:12:44,193
- We can't do that, either.

1629
01:12:44,193 --> 01:12:46,857
The charges won't last
more than a few hours.

1630
01:12:46,857 --> 01:12:48,736
- [Clem] 150 miles.

1631
01:12:48,736 --> 01:12:49,639
- [Crow] Let's beat it.

1632
01:12:49,639 --> 01:12:51,042
- But it's over 200
miles back to Far Side 5.

1633
01:12:51,042 --> 01:12:51,875
- [Crow] My charge is wearing out, too.

1634
01:12:51,875 --> 01:12:54,257
- By track, but this thing's
build to go over mountains.

1635
01:12:54,257 --> 01:12:55,248
- [Joel] Oh, he always
says that when he gets

1636
01:12:55,248 --> 01:12:57,331
in trouble and gets lost.

1637
01:13:02,677 --> 01:13:04,927
(thumping)

1638
01:13:10,102 --> 01:13:11,857
- Boy Servo, what a great movie.

1639
01:13:11,857 --> 01:13:13,028
Great actresses, too.

1640
01:13:13,028 --> 01:13:15,411
I like the babe with the great haircut.

1641
01:13:15,411 --> 01:13:17,905
- The one with Eddie Munster pageboy look?

1642
01:13:17,905 --> 01:13:19,849
Nah, I like the gator-headed chick.

1643
01:13:19,849 --> 01:13:21,299
- The one with the eel skin skull cap?

1644
01:13:21,299 --> 01:13:22,719
No way, ugh.

1645
01:13:22,719 --> 01:13:23,552
- Hey, watch it!

1646
01:13:23,552 --> 01:13:25,131
You're talking about
the woman I love here.

1647
01:13:25,131 --> 01:13:27,102
She's got style, she's got looks,

1648
01:13:27,102 --> 01:13:29,118
she's got a 45 strapped to each thigh

1649
01:13:29,118 --> 01:13:30,891
and she knows how to use them.

1650
01:13:30,891 --> 01:13:31,765
She's a darn sight better

1651
01:13:31,765 --> 01:13:34,669
than that twiggy little stick
woman you have your eyes on.

1652
01:13:34,669 --> 01:13:36,066
- Hey, what are ya sayin'?

1653
01:13:36,066 --> 01:13:38,595
- All I'm saying is that
you little thin-limbed,

1654
01:13:38,595 --> 01:13:42,510
scrawny, empty-headed types
seem to stick together so well.

1655
01:13:42,510 --> 01:13:43,839
- Oh, you want a piece of me?

1656
01:13:43,839 --> 01:13:45,021
I'm standing right here.

1657
01:13:45,021 --> 01:13:48,505
When this is over, you and
me going round and round,

1658
01:13:48,505 --> 01:13:50,027
botto-a-botto.

1659
01:13:50,027 --> 01:13:51,097
- Hey, come on you guys.

1660
01:13:51,097 --> 01:13:51,930
What's goin' on?

1661
01:13:51,930 --> 01:13:53,087
Break it up here.

1662
01:13:53,087 --> 01:13:54,330
What's the problem?

1663
01:13:54,330 --> 01:13:55,409
- Stay out of this, Joel.

1664
01:13:55,409 --> 01:13:58,019
This is between me and the fire plug.

1665
01:13:58,019 --> 01:13:58,965
- That's it, that's it.

1666
01:13:58,965 --> 01:14:01,036
Stand back, Joel, you're using my oxygen.

1667
01:14:01,036 --> 01:14:02,214
Come on, let's go.

1668
01:14:02,214 --> 01:14:03,870
- Listen you guys, if
there's gonna be a fight,

1669
01:14:03,870 --> 01:14:06,688
we're gonna do it my way.

1670
01:14:06,688 --> 01:14:07,696
- Not the.

1671
01:14:07,696 --> 01:14:09,696
(discordant orchestral music)

1672
01:14:15,616 --> 01:14:16,699
I'll get you.

1673
01:14:19,432 --> 01:14:21,765
I want your head on a plate.

1674
01:14:25,325 --> 01:14:27,325
(discordant orchestral music)

1675
01:14:38,391 --> 01:14:40,308
Stay out of this, Joel.

1676
01:14:44,189 --> 01:14:45,189
Turn it off.

1677
01:14:48,022 --> 01:14:50,022
(discordant orchestral music)

1678
01:14:52,001 --> 01:14:53,965
(shouts)

1679
01:14:53,965 --> 01:14:56,709
- Kids, when you got a
beef, don't take the law

1680
01:14:56,709 --> 01:14:59,959
into your own hands, take it to zero G.

1681
01:15:00,903 --> 01:15:05,070
Anti-gravity, it's not
just for breakfast anymore.

1682
01:15:06,978 --> 01:15:10,811
("Moon Zero Two" theme music)

1683
01:15:14,351 --> 01:15:17,169
- We're gonna make it.

1684
01:15:17,169 --> 01:15:21,336
- [Tom] What, are the
driving through snack canyon?

1685
01:15:26,025 --> 01:15:27,775
- [Crow] Hah, on you.

1686
01:15:29,445 --> 01:15:31,445
(jazz music)

1687
01:15:36,579 --> 01:15:39,083
- This is where it starts.

1688
01:15:39,083 --> 01:15:41,500
It'll take a little time yet.

1689
01:15:43,020 --> 01:15:45,103
- [Crow] Automatic pilot.

1690
01:15:52,472 --> 01:15:53,657
- [Tom] Boy, I must have the zactlies,

1691
01:15:53,657 --> 01:15:55,657
my suit's pretty steamy.

1692
01:15:56,877 --> 01:15:58,235
- Well, you better turn
your insulation off

1693
01:15:58,235 --> 01:15:59,869
to save the last of the charge.

1694
01:15:59,869 --> 01:16:02,036
We might need it later on.

1695
01:16:05,946 --> 01:16:08,350
- [Joel] Ow, there's hair back there.

1696
01:16:08,350 --> 01:16:09,517
- How far now.

1697
01:16:13,632 --> 01:16:15,172
- About 80 miles.

1698
01:16:15,172 --> 01:16:16,814
It looks as though the worst is over.

1699
01:16:16,814 --> 01:16:18,957
It seems pretty flat from here.

1700
01:16:18,957 --> 01:16:21,457
- Just when we need the shade.

1701
01:16:25,683 --> 01:16:27,051
Take off your Moon suit.

1702
01:16:27,051 --> 01:16:28,620
- [Crow] He asked her, he asked her.

1703
01:16:28,620 --> 01:16:30,453
Boy, I wish I was him.

1704
01:16:31,999 --> 01:16:34,264
- What are you gonna do now?

1705
01:16:34,264 --> 01:16:35,882
Now, that your not gonna
work with your brother.

1706
01:16:35,882 --> 01:16:37,979
- [Tom] I'm goin' to Disney World.

1707
01:16:37,979 --> 01:16:42,948
- [Crow] Who said she's not
working with her brother?

1708
01:16:42,948 --> 01:16:46,081
- Any jobs going for a good
space shipping clerk up here?

1709
01:16:46,081 --> 01:16:46,914
- Could be.

1710
01:16:46,914 --> 01:16:49,581
- [Crow] Oh, that's a bad thing.

1711
01:16:53,727 --> 01:16:55,142
- Thanks.

1712
01:16:55,142 --> 01:16:57,214
- [Joel] Oh, that feels good, thanks.

1713
01:16:57,214 --> 01:17:00,547
- I'm sorry I dragged you into all this.

1714
01:17:01,491 --> 01:17:02,324
- I'm not.

1715
01:17:03,477 --> 01:17:04,814
- It'd be a bit silly for Bill Kemp

1716
01:17:04,814 --> 01:17:07,314
to die just here, on the Moon.

1717
01:17:08,227 --> 01:17:11,026
After all the places you've been, I mean.

1718
01:17:11,026 --> 01:17:12,891
- It would be a bit silly
for Bill Kemp to die

1719
01:17:12,891 --> 01:17:15,035
any place, as far as I'm concerned.

1720
01:17:15,035 --> 01:17:17,374
So, sit down and start telling me

1721
01:17:17,374 --> 01:17:19,957
how much farther we have to go.

1722
01:17:23,986 --> 01:17:26,874
- [Crow] Gotta get past
his attitude first.

1723
01:17:26,874 --> 01:17:27,966
- [Tom] Well Joel, give me a hand.

1724
01:17:27,966 --> 01:17:28,982
I'm shorting out here.

1725
01:17:28,982 --> 01:17:29,815
What is this?

1726
01:17:29,815 --> 01:17:30,722
- [Joel] Oh, it's just the film.

1727
01:17:30,722 --> 01:17:31,663
Don't worry Servo.

1728
01:17:31,663 --> 01:17:32,913
- [Crow] Focus.

1729
01:17:34,636 --> 01:17:35,977
- [Joel] Oh honey, could
you put some more water

1730
01:17:35,977 --> 01:17:37,227
on those rocks?

1731
01:17:39,576 --> 01:17:42,664
- [Tom] Whoo, nothin' better than this.

1732
01:17:42,664 --> 01:17:43,793
This is the life.

1733
01:17:43,793 --> 01:17:45,328
- Is there any water left?

1734
01:17:45,328 --> 01:17:49,078
- [Crow] Just the gallons
you're putting out.

1735
01:17:55,608 --> 01:17:56,775
Chinese again.

1736
01:18:03,027 --> 01:18:04,370
- [Tom] That's my specimen.

1737
01:18:04,370 --> 01:18:06,453
- It's damn near boiling.

1738
01:18:12,519 --> 01:18:13,938
- Well, what's the matter?

1739
01:18:13,938 --> 01:18:14,771
- [Joel] Yowsa.

1740
01:18:14,771 --> 01:18:16,299
- [Crow] Nothin' from where I'm standing.

1741
01:18:16,299 --> 01:18:18,883
(whistles)

1742
01:18:18,883 --> 01:18:20,050
- Not a thing.

1743
01:18:21,179 --> 01:18:22,726
- [Tom] I knew there had to
be a good part of this movie.

1744
01:18:22,726 --> 01:18:23,662
- If it gets any hotter,

1745
01:18:23,662 --> 01:18:26,265
I'll very likely take the rest of it off.

1746
01:18:26,265 --> 01:18:27,681
- [Joel] Yeah, pray for heat, huh?

1747
01:18:27,681 --> 01:18:28,514
- [Crow] Yeah.
- [Tom] Yeah.

1748
01:18:28,514 --> 01:18:30,681
(buzzing)

1749
01:18:35,159 --> 01:18:36,628
- How much farther?

1750
01:18:36,628 --> 01:18:40,628
- [Joel] Well, the script
says a few more miles.

1751
01:18:42,188 --> 01:18:43,764
- Only a few miles.

1752
01:18:43,764 --> 01:18:45,264
- [Bill] How many?

1753
01:18:46,432 --> 01:18:48,312
- I don't know, seven, ten.

1754
01:18:48,312 --> 01:18:49,185
- Which is it, seven or ten?

1755
01:18:49,185 --> 01:18:50,485
(trumpet blares)

1756
01:18:50,485 --> 01:18:52,043
I'm sorry, but seven
means we might make it

1757
01:18:52,043 --> 01:18:53,304
but ten means we won't.

1758
01:18:53,304 --> 01:18:54,367
- [Crow] What's the trumpet mean?

1759
01:18:54,367 --> 01:18:55,200
- [Bill] Look, we've got power

1760
01:18:55,200 --> 01:18:56,617
for another couple of miles, then we walk.

1761
01:18:56,617 --> 01:18:59,830
We've only got an hours insulation
charge left in the suits.

1762
01:18:59,830 --> 01:19:02,580
(trumpet blares)

1763
01:19:05,045 --> 01:19:07,295
(clicking)

1764
01:19:08,350 --> 01:19:09,839
- [Joel] Shut up that jazz combo,

1765
01:19:09,839 --> 01:19:11,338
I'm nervous enough as it is.

1766
01:19:11,338 --> 01:19:14,280
- The overheating has
melted the pressure button.

1767
01:19:14,280 --> 01:19:15,280
I only hope.

1768
01:19:17,205 --> 01:19:18,945
(beeping)

1769
01:19:18,945 --> 01:19:20,379
Quick, get into your suit!

1770
01:19:20,379 --> 01:19:24,546
- [Tom] Oh damn, I thought we
were taking those suits off.

1771
01:19:25,613 --> 01:19:27,613
(suspenseful orchestral music)

1772
01:19:30,981 --> 01:19:32,814
- [Joel] Suicide door.

1773
01:19:35,237 --> 01:19:37,680
- [Tom] Those exploder
bolts come in handy.

1774
01:19:37,680 --> 01:19:38,865
- [Joel] Of course it's gonna explode.

1775
01:19:38,865 --> 01:19:41,198
They parked over a campfire.

1776
01:19:42,076 --> 01:19:44,076
(suspenseful orchestral music)

1777
01:19:45,432 --> 01:19:48,409
- [Crow] Quick, in the crack.

1778
01:19:48,409 --> 01:19:49,242
(explosions)

1779
01:19:49,242 --> 01:19:51,702
Wait, oh, the jazz
combo is still in there.

1780
01:19:51,702 --> 01:19:54,285
- [Tom] The day the music died.

1781
01:19:55,787 --> 01:19:59,204
- [Crow] Guess they won't try that again.

1782
01:20:01,051 --> 01:20:03,235
- [Joel] Who decided free
form jazz was the right thing

1783
01:20:03,235 --> 01:20:04,818
for the soundtrack?

1784
01:20:08,374 --> 01:20:09,207
- Look.

1785
01:20:12,447 --> 01:20:15,712
- [Bill] It's Far Side 5, come on.

1786
01:20:15,712 --> 01:20:18,438
- [Tom] They make a great
burger, let's beat cheeks.

1787
01:20:18,438 --> 01:20:20,006
- [Liz] All right, Bill,
you're under arrest.

1788
01:20:20,006 --> 01:20:21,839
- [Tom] Beat the rush.

1789
01:20:23,050 --> 01:20:26,967
- Fine, now has anybody
got a nice, cool drink?

1790
01:20:27,879 --> 01:20:29,632
- [Tom] Don't say hi.

1791
01:20:29,632 --> 01:20:32,549
- [Crow] Hope he likes rocket fuel.

1792
01:20:38,460 --> 01:20:41,265
- [Joel] She just flopped
down on that Formica.

1793
01:20:41,265 --> 01:20:43,678
- Just suddenly appeared.

1794
01:20:43,678 --> 01:20:46,095
She's always gets her man, B.

1795
01:20:48,258 --> 01:20:51,792
- Well, you've really been
running up an account, this time.

1796
01:20:51,792 --> 01:20:53,470
- [Crow] And you're the
one with the funny skull.

1797
01:20:53,470 --> 01:20:55,744
- [Liz] Liable to breach the
pressure of an outside room.

1798
01:20:55,744 --> 01:20:57,975
Malicious damage to hotel property.

1799
01:20:57,975 --> 01:21:01,815
- Miss Taplin's brother has
been murdered out there.

1800
01:21:01,815 --> 01:21:05,065
- Come on, you can do better than that.

1801
01:21:06,889 --> 01:21:08,351
- Yes, well, three other
characters got killed.

1802
01:21:08,351 --> 01:21:10,309
But I did that myself.

1803
01:21:10,309 --> 01:21:11,736
- [Liz] When you get into trouble,

1804
01:21:11,736 --> 01:21:14,809
you really jump off the
top board, don't you?

1805
01:21:14,809 --> 01:21:16,174
- [Tom] She's really got
sort of a Julie Andrews

1806
01:21:16,174 --> 01:21:17,512
quality, doesn't she?

1807
01:21:17,512 --> 01:21:19,099
- [Joel] Yeah, I think
she got that skull cap

1808
01:21:19,099 --> 01:21:21,599
from Thoroughly Modern Millie.

1809
01:21:23,411 --> 01:21:26,225
- One thing we can check right now.

1810
01:21:26,225 --> 01:21:29,725
Wally Taplin was killed with one of these.

1811
01:21:31,313 --> 01:21:33,832
Tell me what you think it smells like?

1812
01:21:33,832 --> 01:21:34,840
- [Crow] Cheese balls.

1813
01:21:34,840 --> 01:21:35,789
- [Tom] Pine forest.

1814
01:21:35,789 --> 01:21:36,622
- [Joel] Watermelon?

1815
01:21:36,622 --> 01:21:37,802
- [Crow] Wet canvas.

1816
01:21:37,802 --> 01:21:39,219
- Just one sniff.

1817
01:21:40,727 --> 01:21:42,237
- It could be cyanide.

1818
01:21:42,237 --> 01:21:45,999
- [Tom] Cyanide, that's my favorite.

1819
01:21:45,999 --> 01:21:48,251
- Funny, I just took it off my own suit.

1820
01:21:48,251 --> 01:21:50,501
- [Joel] Smells like me, and I didn't like

1821
01:21:50,501 --> 01:21:52,918
that wet canvas crack either.

1822
01:21:55,227 --> 01:21:57,998
- [Bill] But you thought it was cyanide.

1823
01:21:57,998 --> 01:21:59,339
Now, you sold him this air bottle.

1824
01:21:59,339 --> 01:22:01,705
You sell all the air bottles around here.

1825
01:22:01,705 --> 01:22:03,253
- [Crow] Yeah, but I distinctly remember

1826
01:22:03,253 --> 01:22:05,258
not selling that air bottle.

1827
01:22:05,258 --> 01:22:07,921
- You'd better answer him.

1828
01:22:07,921 --> 01:22:09,381
- They made me.

1829
01:22:09,381 --> 01:22:11,169
They wanted Taplin's claim.

1830
01:22:11,169 --> 01:22:13,152
It was due to expire.

1831
01:22:13,152 --> 01:22:16,565
It seems they'd found something,
and they had to have it.

1832
01:22:16,565 --> 01:22:17,482
- What for?

1833
01:22:21,542 --> 01:22:22,375
- [Tom] Tell 'em.

1834
01:22:22,375 --> 01:22:24,708
- To land an asteroid on it.

1835
01:22:26,871 --> 01:22:29,315
- [Joel] Pizza's ready.

1836
01:22:29,315 --> 01:22:31,732
- [Tom] It's a brand new car.

1837
01:22:36,836 --> 01:22:39,098
- Mr. Hubbard, they found out about it.

1838
01:22:39,098 --> 01:22:40,827
You've got to get me off the Moon.

1839
01:22:40,827 --> 01:22:42,976
- [Crow] Hogan, I wouldn't
say anymore about that

1840
01:22:42,976 --> 01:22:44,213
if I were you.

1841
01:22:44,213 --> 01:22:48,061
- So, you've reached the confession stage.

1842
01:22:48,061 --> 01:22:51,952
And in front of the
Bureau of Investigation.

1843
01:22:51,952 --> 01:22:54,039
- [Tom] Is that wrong,
should I have not done that?

1844
01:22:54,039 --> 01:22:55,889
- I'm glad we got here on time, Mr. Kemp.

1845
01:22:55,889 --> 01:22:57,724
I was afraid some of my associates might.

1846
01:22:57,724 --> 01:22:58,988
- [Joel] Never trust a man with a collar

1847
01:22:58,988 --> 01:23:01,078
made of shinyl vinyl.

1848
01:23:01,078 --> 01:23:03,697
- [Hubbard] To this project,
and would have disposed of you.

1849
01:23:03,697 --> 01:23:05,277
- [Bill] They tried.

1850
01:23:05,277 --> 01:23:07,849
- [Hubbard] Really, all three of them?

1851
01:23:07,849 --> 01:23:09,125
- All three.

1852
01:23:09,125 --> 01:23:10,389
- [Crow] Even raspberry red?

1853
01:23:10,389 --> 01:23:11,737
(laughs)

1854
01:23:11,737 --> 01:23:15,917
- I really did
underestimate you, Mr. Kemp.

1855
01:23:15,917 --> 01:23:18,500
So, we're three men short then.

1856
01:23:19,851 --> 01:23:21,340
Whitsun, make a note to engage.

1857
01:23:21,340 --> 01:23:24,757
- [Liz] Mr. Hubbard, you're under arrest.

1858
01:23:25,919 --> 01:23:27,745
- I don't think so.

1859
01:23:27,745 --> 01:23:29,738
Do you, Harry?

1860
01:23:29,738 --> 01:23:33,905
- [Crow] Uh, I'm sorry, I
was dreaming about rabbits.

1861
01:23:35,625 --> 01:23:37,015
- Let me get this straight.

1862
01:23:37,015 --> 01:23:39,021
You got that louse to murder my brother,

1863
01:23:39,021 --> 01:23:41,284
just so that you could have
a place to land an asteroid.

1864
01:23:41,284 --> 01:23:42,700
- [Joel] Or in a nutshell, yeah.

1865
01:23:42,700 --> 01:23:44,496
- Oh, stuff it.

1866
01:23:44,496 --> 01:23:47,079
- [Joel] Speaking of nutshells.

1867
01:23:48,000 --> 01:23:50,333
(gun fires)

1868
01:23:52,456 --> 01:23:53,531
(gun fires)

1869
01:23:53,531 --> 01:23:55,109
- [Tom] Oop, well I'll get that.

1870
01:23:55,109 --> 01:23:56,113
That's okay, no problem.

1871
01:23:56,113 --> 01:23:57,698
- [Hubbard] Mr. Kemp, please,

1872
01:23:57,698 --> 01:24:00,522
don't get yourself killed unnecessarily.

1873
01:24:00,522 --> 01:24:01,555
- [Crow] Oh, okay.

1874
01:24:01,555 --> 01:24:03,888
Hey, are those French cuffs?

1875
01:24:07,161 --> 01:24:10,381
- [Joel] I suppose I look
rather silly to you, Bill.

1876
01:24:10,381 --> 01:24:13,324
- I don't think I'm gonna be able

1877
01:24:13,324 --> 01:24:16,292
to get you out of this
one this time, Bill.

1878
01:24:16,292 --> 01:24:17,792
- Don't talk, Liz.

1879
01:24:19,398 --> 01:24:22,148
- [Liz] I'm gonna miss you, Bill.

1880
01:24:23,760 --> 01:24:25,760
Even the rows were good.

1881
01:24:29,049 --> 01:24:32,799
- [Joel] Bury my turtle
head at Wounded Knee.

1882
01:24:35,730 --> 01:24:36,563
- We're quite safe, sir.

1883
01:24:36,563 --> 01:24:38,896
None of the shots pierced the Devin.

1884
01:24:38,896 --> 01:24:40,464
- Yes, well, I believe we need to take off

1885
01:24:40,464 --> 01:24:43,797
for the asteroid in just under one hour.

1886
01:24:44,766 --> 01:24:46,783
- If you think I'm gonna land that thing

1887
01:24:46,783 --> 01:24:47,727
for you now.

1888
01:24:47,727 --> 01:24:48,644
- But I do.

1889
01:24:50,369 --> 01:24:53,817
Whitsun, when was the last
time we abandoned a project

1890
01:24:53,817 --> 01:24:54,920
without any profit?

1891
01:24:54,920 --> 01:24:55,753
- Antarctica, sir.

1892
01:24:55,753 --> 01:24:56,586
- [Tom] Stonehenge.

1893
01:24:56,586 --> 01:24:57,419
- Oh yes, I remember.

1894
01:24:57,419 --> 01:24:58,578
There was no oil there, was there?

1895
01:24:58,578 --> 01:25:02,546
No, but there is sapphire in
this asteroid, Mr. Kemp, so.

1896
01:25:02,546 --> 01:25:04,429
- [Crow] If she's dead,
I could scoop her out

1897
01:25:04,429 --> 01:25:07,096
and make a terrarium out of her.

1898
01:25:09,959 --> 01:25:13,445
- [Joel] Somebody say something.

1899
01:25:13,445 --> 01:25:15,945
- Harry, shoot the young lady.

1900
01:25:17,445 --> 01:25:19,231
- [Crow] Uh, which one?

1901
01:25:19,231 --> 01:25:21,377
- [Joel] Ah, the one
that's not dead, you idiot.

1902
01:25:21,377 --> 01:25:25,369
- [Crow] Oh, yeah, yeah,
yeah, right, right.

1903
01:25:25,369 --> 01:25:28,119
Huh? All right, okay, don't move.

1904
01:25:40,149 --> 01:25:41,726
- That's better.

1905
01:25:41,726 --> 01:25:42,809
- [Tom] Much.

1906
01:25:46,698 --> 01:25:50,531
("Moon Zero Two" theme music)

1907
01:25:53,755 --> 01:25:56,172
- $200,000 in the gem market.

1908
01:25:58,951 --> 01:26:02,284
- [Joel] You kids be careful down there.

1909
01:26:03,152 --> 01:26:05,338
- We can arrange that by
firing two engines early.

1910
01:26:05,338 --> 01:26:07,287
- One of them will have
to be number three.

1911
01:26:07,287 --> 01:26:08,465
Just remember that.

1912
01:26:08,465 --> 01:26:09,641
- [Crow] Yeah, yeah, yeah, I knew that.

1913
01:26:09,641 --> 01:26:11,898
- I anticipated that.

1914
01:26:11,898 --> 01:26:12,873
- [Tom] Got my gum back.

1915
01:26:12,873 --> 01:26:16,094
- [Whitsun] Number three controls
the firing of the others.

1916
01:26:16,094 --> 01:26:19,702
- Come out here where the
boyfriend can see you, love.

1917
01:26:19,702 --> 01:26:22,535
Sort of encourage him in his work.

1918
01:26:25,015 --> 01:26:25,848
- [Joel] That's it, Bill.

1919
01:26:25,848 --> 01:26:26,681
Keep it up, buddy.

1920
01:26:26,681 --> 01:26:28,431
You're looking great.

1921
01:26:29,921 --> 01:26:33,444
- At four and seven minutes,
it'll still land on the claim.

1922
01:26:33,444 --> 01:26:36,360
- [Crow] Now, could someone
get me a medium sherry.

1923
01:26:36,360 --> 01:26:38,384
A very nice one, thank you.

1924
01:26:38,384 --> 01:26:41,059
- If I'm gonna stay down here
and thump number three again,

1925
01:26:41,059 --> 01:26:42,603
- [Crow] I'll need a magazine.

1926
01:26:42,603 --> 01:26:44,708
I'll need a long line.

1927
01:26:44,708 --> 01:26:46,049
- [Tom] Like a soliloquy or a monologue?

1928
01:26:46,049 --> 01:26:47,216
- Miss Taplin.

1929
01:26:49,278 --> 01:26:52,222
- [Bill] There up in the passenger deck.

1930
01:26:52,222 --> 01:26:55,972
- [Tom] Should I make
coffee too, Mr. Hefner?

1931
01:26:58,654 --> 01:27:00,310
- [Crow] This is one of my
best escape tricks ever.

1932
01:27:00,310 --> 01:27:04,227
I chain myself to this
ladder, see, and oh, hi.

1933
01:27:14,464 --> 01:27:16,375
- What can I do?

1934
01:27:16,375 --> 01:27:19,715
- Enter the control deck, use chair.

1935
01:27:19,715 --> 01:27:22,083
- [Joel] Deck, you mean.

1936
01:27:22,083 --> 01:27:24,671
- I think that's everything, sir.

1937
01:27:24,671 --> 01:27:27,875
It will land 16 minutes after firing.

1938
01:27:27,875 --> 01:27:29,108
- [Crow] Give or take a
millionth of a second.

1939
01:27:29,108 --> 01:27:31,428
- Excellent timing, Whitsun.

1940
01:27:31,428 --> 01:27:35,447
Mr. Taplin's claim
expired half an hour ago.

1941
01:27:35,447 --> 01:27:36,947
No legal problems.

1942
01:27:39,800 --> 01:27:41,477
- [Karminski] Are you there?

1943
01:27:41,477 --> 01:27:42,360
- Yes.

1944
01:27:42,360 --> 01:27:46,301
- [Karminski] On your
right, two rows of switches.

1945
01:27:46,301 --> 01:27:47,218
Bottom row.

1946
01:27:49,829 --> 01:27:52,872
- [Crow] Has that guy ever
spoken a complete sentence?

1947
01:27:52,872 --> 01:27:54,528
- [Karminski] All right?

1948
01:27:54,528 --> 01:27:55,716
- What now?

1949
01:27:55,716 --> 01:28:00,189
- [Karminski] On your
left, the big front level,

1950
01:28:00,189 --> 01:28:02,429
pull it all the way back.

1951
01:28:02,429 --> 01:28:05,904
- [Joel] But before you do that,

1952
01:28:05,904 --> 01:28:07,112
- Now.

1953
01:28:07,112 --> 01:28:09,112
(suspenseful orchestral music)

1954
01:28:12,611 --> 01:28:14,528
- [Tom] Oh, no smoking
in your pressure suit.

1955
01:28:14,528 --> 01:28:16,445
First rule in the book.

1956
01:28:18,334 --> 01:28:20,224
- [Crow] Hot head.

1957
01:28:20,224 --> 01:28:22,574
- [Joel] I bet he won't try that again.

1958
01:28:22,574 --> 01:28:25,073
- Cut the thrust.

1959
01:28:25,073 --> 01:28:27,290
- the engineer must have got free.

1960
01:28:27,290 --> 01:28:28,426
- [Tom] Yes. (snorts)

1961
01:28:28,426 --> 01:28:29,305
- But he can't have.

1962
01:28:29,305 --> 01:28:31,697
- Of course, you think the
girl is flying that thing.

1963
01:28:31,697 --> 01:28:35,016
- In the maintenance locker
there, there is a buzz saw.

1964
01:28:35,016 --> 01:28:39,760
- Mr. Kemp, can your engineer
land that thing on his own?

1965
01:28:39,760 --> 01:28:41,349
- [Joel] Well, he's not
good at parallel parking,

1966
01:28:41,349 --> 01:28:44,409
but if it's a diagonal spot, he can do it.

1967
01:28:44,409 --> 01:28:45,769
- No, I don't think so.

1968
01:28:45,769 --> 01:28:50,674
- Then, he needs you as
much as we need the ship.

1969
01:28:50,674 --> 01:28:51,923
- [Crow] And vice versa.

1970
01:28:51,923 --> 01:28:55,340
- [Hubbard] Mr. Kaminski, if you hear me,

1971
01:28:56,581 --> 01:28:57,623
we are ready to go home now.

1972
01:28:57,623 --> 01:29:00,956
- [Tom] He could have just grabbed that.

1973
01:29:01,808 --> 01:29:03,066
- Go get him, Harry.

1974
01:29:03,066 --> 01:29:06,396
- That's right, Harry, come and get me.

1975
01:29:06,396 --> 01:29:07,910
- He's got a gun, Mr. Hubbard.

1976
01:29:07,910 --> 01:29:10,327
- I know that, it's your gun.

1977
01:29:11,506 --> 01:29:14,008
Just start earning your pay, for once.

1978
01:29:14,008 --> 01:29:18,175
